{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv && rg -n 'redirect|if_stage|hazard_unit|imem bus' cores/bench\"", "aggregated_output": "// rtl/if_stage.sv\n//\n// Instruction fetch stage. Holds the PC register; the IF/ID payload\n// (pc + instr + valid) is *combinational* — there is no separate IF/ID\n// flop in this microarchitecture, the next-stage's ID/EX register\n// captures everything one cycle later.\n//\n// On flush or redirect, the instruction emitted to ID is forced to NOP\n// (`0x00000013` = ADDI x0,x0,0). This prevents the hazard unit from\n// observing a real rs1/rs2 from a wrong-path instruction and inserting\n// a spurious load-use stall the cycle after a taken branch.\n//\n// Latency:        PC-reg update is synchronous; output is combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds pc_rdata (via ID/EX/MEM/WB) and pc_wdata (via\n//                 EX-stage redirect).\nmodule if_stage (\n  input  logic              clock,\n  input  logic              reset,\n  input  logic              stall,            // hold PC (load-use)\n  input  logic              flush,            // emit NOP into ID this cycle\n  input  logic              redirect,         // EX has resolved a branch/jump\n  input  logic [31:0]       redirect_target,\n  output logic [31:0]       imem_addr,\n  input  logic [31:0]       imem_data,\n  output if_id_t  out\n);\n\n  localparam logic [31:0] RESET_PC = 32'h0000_0000;\n\n  logic [31:0] pc;\n  logic [31:0] next_pc;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    next_pc = redirect ? redirect_target : pc + 32'd4;\n  end\n\n  // Redirect must override stall: a BRANCH/JAL/JALR in EX may fire\n  // redirect on the same cycle as imem_stall or dmem_stall — without\n  // this priority the redirect target would be silently dropped, the\n  // PC would hold its old (wrong-path) value, and execution would\n  // resume on the wrong path once the bus unstalls. Verified by the\n  // VexRiscv-binary CoreMark sweep with --istall enabled.\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if      (reset)    pc <= RESET_PC;\n    else if (redirect) pc <= redirect_target;\n    else if (!stall)   pc <= next_pc;\n  end\n\n  assign imem_addr = pc;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    out.pc    = pc;\n    out.instr = (flush || redirect) ? 32\n…[61150 of 64222 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:dd0d5b15dac60ef5]…\nration validated.` The start and stop markers were at cycles 15,185 and 4,206,526, giving **4,191,341 cycles** for 10 iterations.\\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 45.808, 49.492, and 49.492 MHz, respectively. Option 0 used 3,455 LUT4s and 30 LUT-RAM blocks. The worst setup paths still passed through the ALU. The **49.492 MHz median** gives **118.08 iter/s**, 15.5% above the stated 102.23 iter/s baseline.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T20:35:28.583102Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +15.50%): With EX-to-BTB training registered on the clocked-divider core, the ALU path limited median Fmax to 49.492 MHz, below the 51.64 MHz the 32-entry BTB needed for its 300,144-cycle savings on stalled CoreMark to beat the round\\u2019s register-file and forwarding optimization.\"}\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:464:            \"if_stage.sv\", \"id_stage.sv\", \"ex_stage.sv\",\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:466:            \"hazard_unit.sv\", \"forward_unit.sv\",\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
