ow the 51.64 MHz the 32-entry BTB needed for its 300,144-cycle savings on stalled CoreMark to beat the round’s register-file and forwarding optimization.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +9.49%): With combinational IF/ID, feeding the resolved EX target to imem and the decode PC removed the redirect bubble on accepted fetches and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.91%, with the remaining EX-to-dmem critical path limiting throughput to 134.90 iterations/s against the winning execute-path optimization’s 138.68 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 (improvement, +12.56%): With the effective address registered separately at EX/MEM, parallel threshold subtraction in two radix-8 digits cut the divider’s serial subtract-and-select depth from three stages to two at six bits per cycle, helping raise median Fmax from 55.338 to 62.290 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (regression, +7.99%): Registering the selected writeback data in MEM/WB and moving AUIPC to the PC-relative adder shifted the 64-entry BTB core’s critical path from WB forwarding through the ALU to the divider, and at the resulting 55.699 MHz median, saving 305,155 stalled CoreMark cycles yielded 133.05 iter/s against the shorter-divider variant’s 138.68 iter/s.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 (regression, +20.03%): Seeding the remainder with the leading dividend byte enabled one radix-16 digit per cycle for divisor magnitudes of at least 256, and using two 11-bit radix-8 trials per cycle for smaller divisors preserved the six-cycle recurrence while shifting the timing limit to EX result forwarding through the ALU into EX/MEM at a median of 74.765 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 (regression, +11.16%): Forming B/J targets from instruction immediates in IF let a 128-entry direction table eliminate BTB tag and target arrays and save 289,213 stalled CoreMark cycles, with the divider-limited 64.783 MHz median falling short of the 74.