026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +15.50%): With EX-to-BTB training registered on the clocked-divider core, the ALU path limited median Fmax to 49.492 MHz, below the 51.64 MHz the 32-entry BTB needed for its 300,144-cycle savings on stalled CoreMark to beat the round’s register-file and forwarding optimization.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp\n…[7330 of 10402 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:68083314c7f8db41]…\n stalled CoreMark cycles, but\\nits measured 55.699 MHz median on the previous parent erased the gain\\nagainst the curren\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Fetch resolved direct targets and register the PC selection\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion reaches 176.19 CoreMark iter/s at 79.136 MHz\\nmedian and 4,491,485 stalled marker cycles. The earlier immediate\\nredirect experiment cut \n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Share fetch and execute PC state for same-cycle redirects\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 176.19 CoreMark iterations/s at a 79.136 MHz Gowin median and 4,491,485 marker cycles. Taken control transfers discard the sequenti\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Predict transfers with a region-relative BTB and registered recovery\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The earlier 32-entry BTB saved 300,144 stalled CoreMark cycles by\\npredicting both direct and indirect transfers. Its second asynchronous\\nmetadata\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
