{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_254","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short && diff -qr cores/bench/rtl '/srv/hwebench/tmp/5a1ea1b1/7b44e8c9/bench-final-verify.HzTUkz48/cores/bench/rtl' && cmp cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py '/srv/hwebench/tmp/5a1ea1b1/7b44e8c9/bench-final-verify.HzTUkz48/cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py' && sed -n '1,150p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Sequential fetch buffering\n\nIF uses a two-word fall-through FIFO with independent head and tail valid bits. Each word carries a B, JAL, or JALR opcode predecode. The selected PC bank tracks the next word to issue, and the imem request looks ahead by the FIFO occupancy. Admitting a possible transfer clears the queue, and a valid branch or jump in EX blocks refill. Direct targets still reach decode in the resolution cycle when imem is ready, and JALR still redirects through the registered PC after squashing decode.\n\nThe hazard unit checks load-use tags on the valid instruction offered to decode. A dmem stall holds ID/EX and EX/MEM and clears MEM/WB retirement validity while preserving forwarding data. The directed tests check store request stability, two-word capacity, full-queue replacement, issue with imem stalled, load-use interlocking, and direct-target acceptance and retry. They also check accepted data transactions and exact RVFI retirement PCs and order.\n\nThe issue successor and lookahead address share the increment of the upper PC bits. The carry out of the two low word-address bits selects the incremented upper bits. A full pop promotes the tail and places a ready replacement in the tail. A single-entry pop places any ready replacement directly in the head. The slot valid bits clear together on a possible transfer.\n\n- Verilator lint and all six cocotb runners passed. Cosim passed selftest and three random traces with and without bus stalls, plus the canonical 2K CoreMark CRC and bounds checks.\n- The single-channel ALTOPS formal gate passed all 53 checks on the selected RTL. There were no failed checks or fix attempts.\n- CoreMark completed ten iterations in 4,037,811 marker cycles, a reduction of 110,692. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 79.799, 87.033, and 87.033 MHz. The median yields 215.55 iterations/s, 8.16% above the previous winner and above the 208.46 iterations/s threshold. Execute forwarding limits option 0, and the narrow divider recurrence limits options 1 and 2.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
