{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_30", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git show -s --oneline && ls -la cores/bench/experiments && cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "7367bd7 log: hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 regression\ntotal 208\ndrwxrwx---+ 2 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 20 22:48 .\ndrwxrwx---+ 8 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 20 22:54 ..\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup      0 Nov 20 22:48 .gitkeep\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup 125570 Nov 20 22:48 log.jsonl\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup  66290 Nov 20 22:48 progress.png\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (regression, +742.82%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared five-bit-per-cycle restoring divider shortened the EX critical path and raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 45.881 MHz, while CoreMark stayed at 4,491,485 measured cycles under bus stalls with both DIVU instructions paying their extra latency outside the timed region.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.99%): The 32-entry partial-tag BTB saved 300,144 stalled CoreMark cycles by avoiding EX squashes on correct predictions, while the forwarded-operand ALU critical path limited median Fmax to 5.538 MHz and left throughput below the round’s clocked-divider winner.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (improvement, +20.52%): Asynchronous LUT RAM reads removed BSRAM clock-to-output delay from the multiply and branch operand paths, and registering forwarding tag matches at ID/EX let EX use one-hot operand selection, raising median Fmax from 45.918 to 55.338 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r2s1 (regression, +19.89%): Separately registering the effective address and multiply product at EX/MEM and using valid bits to squash younger instructions removed the multiplier from the BRAM address path and instruction masking from fetch-PC feedback, raising median Fmax to 55.048 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count and exposing the divider recurrence as the worst setup path in placement option 0.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +15.50%): With EX-to-BTB training registered on the clocked-divider core, the ALU path limited median Fmax to 49.492 MHz, below the 51.64 MHz the 32-entry BTB needed for its 300,144-cycle savings on stalled CoreMark to beat the round’s register-file and forwarding optimization.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +9.49%): With combinational IF/ID, feeding the resolved EX target to imem and the decode PC removed the redirect bubble on accepted fetches and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.91%, with the remaining EX-to-dmem critical path limiting throughput to 134.90 iterations/s against the winning execute-path optimization’s 138.68 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 (improvement, +12.56%): With the effective address registered separately at EX/MEM, parallel threshold subtraction in two radix-8 digits cut the divider’s serial subtract-and-select depth from three stages to two at six bits per cycle, helping raise median Fmax from 55.338 to 62.290 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (regression, +7.99%): Registering the selected writeback data in MEM/WB and moving AUIPC to the PC-relative adder shifted the 64-entry BTB core’s critical path from WB forwarding through the ALU to the divider, and at the resulting 55.699 MHz median, saving 305,155 stalled CoreMark cycles yielded 133.05 iter/s against the shorter-divider variant’s 138.68 iter/s.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 (regression, +20.03%): Seeding the remainder with the leading dividend byte enabled one radix-16 digit per cycle for divisor magnitudes of at least 256, and using two 11-bit radix-8 trials per cycle for smaller divisors preserved the six-cycle recurrence while shifting the timing limit to EX result forwarding through the ALU into EX/MEM at a median of 74.765 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 (regression, +11.16%): Forming B/J targets from instruction immediates in IF let a 128-entry direction table eliminate BTB tag and target arrays and save 289,213 stalled CoreMark cycles, with the divider-limited 64.783 MHz median falling short of the 74.04 MHz needed to match the round winner.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
