{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check; sed -n '1,100p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Direct-successor fetch prediction\n\n- IF predecodes aligned JAL and the six conditional branches. A 13-bit sum and sign check constrain predicted targets to the source’s 16 KiB region. The 128-entry bimodal table uses a resettable trained bitmap and applies registered EX updates. Same-edge fetches see the previous state.\n- A four-entry circular FIFO stores flat packed words with the instruction, PC, PC+4 and predicted direction. Queued PCs omit the constant upper bits and retain the PC+4 carry at the fetch boundary. The FIFO bypasses when empty, refills on dequeue and can fetch while load-use, divide or dmem holds block ID.\n- EX registers repairs for direct direction mismatches and aligned JALR. The opposite of the predicted direction selects the direct correction in parallel with the branch compare. IF offers the correction during repair and retries it if necessary. Repair suppresses the younger EX instruction’s divider issue, training and EX/MEM controls.\n- A separate registered advance signal releases a paused fall-through at the final in-window word. An architecturally resolved out-of-window PC retires a synthetic illegal word under the core’s existing trap convention. The external fetch address stays in bounds.\n- Directed cocotb coverage includes queue fill and refill under stalls, buffered load-use and issue, direction recovery, predictor training, wrong-path suppression, region crossings, alignment and the fetch-window boundary.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all 108 cocotb cases passed. Cosimulation passed for selftest and all three random traces with both zero-wait and stalled buses, plus stalled CoreMark. The local simulator build used `NRET=1` from the bench core’s declared retirement width.\n- Stalled canonical CoreMark took 3,980,295 marker cycles, matched all five CRCs and reported no out-of-bounds accesses.\n- The full `formal/run_all.sh` gate passed all 53 single-channel checks on the corrected RTL after one fix. The preceding run found a `reg_ch0` counterexample. A JAL to `0xfff8eb5c` led to a synthetic fetch fault with both RVFI source addresses set to x0 and nonzero data from the parked bus word. The RVFI outputs now report zero data for x0. A directed test supplies a parked word that reads a nonzero register.\n- All three Gowin placements completed on the corrected RTL at 80.621, 85.811 and 85.811 MHz. The median of 85.811 MHz gives 215.59 CoreMark iterations/s, above the 208.46 cutoff. Gowin reported 5,124 logic cells and 1,501 registers. Option 0 alone falls below the frequency required at this cycle count.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_104","type":"agent_message","text":"The four-entry fetch queue passed all 53 formal checks, Verilator lint, 108 cocotb cases, and cosimulation. Stalled CoreMark reached **215.59 iterations/s** at a median **85.811 MHz**, above the 208.46 cutoff.\n\nResolved out-of-window fetches report a synthetic illegal instruction at the full architectural PC. [Implementation notes](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261120-003-r6s2/implementation_notes.md)"}}
