{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path; rs=list(map(json.loads,Path(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").read_text().splitlines())); print(list(rs[-1])); [print(str(r)[-2800:]) for r in rs if r[\\\"id\\\"].endswith((\\\"r9s2\\\",\\\"r10s0\\\",\\\"r11s0\\\",\\\"r12s0\\\"))]'\"", "aggregated_output": "['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson']\n occupancy, the lane index, and the prediction sent to EX. IMEM can fill any free slot during an ID or EX hold.\\n\\nThe training collector reads ordered EX/MEM-to-WB advances and finishes on the fourth word or at a prefix boundary. The update pipeline registers the tag match and counter result before writing the table. Back-to-back updates to the same row forward the pending training result. Fetch reads sample the pre-edge table state for both lookups. A matching not-taken branch changes direction only.\\n\\nRegistered EX repair clears younger work and squashes divider issue. The resolver remains in EX/MEM through the repair edge and can forward its link to the first corrected instruction through MEM/WB. FENCE.I also clears predictor validity and pending training. An out-of-window logical PC uses a parked bus address and retires a synthetic illegal word with the full PC.\\n\\nThe directed pipeline tests cover short-tag alias rejection, an altered source word, replay during imem stalls, direction counter transitions, repair priority over a pending guess and wrong-path divide, FENCE.I invalidation, and fetch-window behavior. They check retirement PCs and order as well as accepted data transactions.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- The initial single-channel formal run found incorrect PC and link results because Yosys treated writes to members of a struct array as implicit scalar wires. Writing the whole packed PC word to each FIFO lane fixed the counterexample, and the rerun passed all 53 checks before the final timing changes. Formal on the final RTL was incomplete at the local wall-clock limit. At the last check, 19 of 53 checks had passe\n…[8528 of 11600 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b4e4336d3c1bfddb]…\nt 10.109 ns, with branch-to-training at 10.086 ns. The data RAM feedback into the effective-address register did not appear among the reported top 25 setup paths for any option.\\n\\nA trial with unconditional repairs folded into the comparison controls and localized predictor word capture measured 92.556, 96.756, and 96.756 MHz. A further trial moved the JALR successor into EX to shorten its fetch path. Its option 0 measured 91.100 MHz and was limited by ordinary EX operand forwarding into the result lane. Its other reports were pending at the end of the local run.\\n\", 'timestamp': '2026-11-21T08:20:45.299263Z', 'slot': 0, 'round_id': 12, 'lesson': '- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r12s0 (regression, +2.49%): With prefix carries for offsets 0–2, LW-to-load forwarding left RAM-to-address feedback outside the top 25 setup paths, with JALR correction into fetch capping median Gowin Fmax at 98.923 MHz, below the 100.962 MHz needed to win after the address and BEQ/BNE bypasses saved 185,459 stalled CoreMark cycles.'}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
