{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_121","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"find formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-pcDispRaw/checks -maxdepth 1 -name '*.sby' | wc -l; git diff --check && git status --short --untracked-files=all && sed -n '1,90p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"53\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Dedicated PC-relative displacement\n\nID compares the encoded BRANCH and JAL halfword displacements against two and qualifies each result with its validated decoder control. Synthetic fetches use the canonical zero word for legality. The compares fold zeroing into the comparison results, and the displacement enables all clear on a synthetic fetch. A 30-bit ID/EX displacement carries the sign-extended branch or jump word offset, or the AUIPC upper immediate shifted by ten.\n\nEX uses the registered word displacement for the parallel target and target-plus-four sums. The ordinary immediate still supplies alignment, JALR, memory addressing, and ALU operations. The target inequality controls repair, while the branch outcome still drives replay training when the target is PC+4.\n\nDirected pipeline tests cover signed nonsequential branch and jump offsets, PC+4 replay and direction updates, misaligned targets, and AUIPC wrap and forwarding.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all six cocotb runners passed.\n- A local SAT check matched the registered inequality to the original immediate comparison and the registered word displacement to the original immediate slice for BRANCH, JAL, and AUIPC, including arbitrary synthetic inputs and stalls.\n- Co-simulation passed for selftest and all three random programs, both with and without stalls. The local Makefile invocation used `NRET=1` to match `core.yaml`.\n- Stalled CoreMark reproduced 3,564,868 marker cycles and all five canonical CRCs.\n- The unchanged single-channel formal gate has reported passes for nine of 53 checks so far, including legality, liveness, and uniqueness. It has reported zero counterexamples and the remaining proofs are pending.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 95.715, 104.413, and 104.413 MHz. The median yields 292.89 CoreMark iterations/s at the measured cycle count and falls below the 106.742 MHz acceptance floor. In the median placements, the worst path runs from the fetch replay source check to FIFO occupancy.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
