{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_41", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'repair|Fmax|divider|timing' cores/bench/LESSONS.md | tail -45; du -sh cores/bench/generated/timing\"", "aggregated_output": "1:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (regression, +742.82%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared five-bit-per-cycle restoring divider shortened the EX critical path and raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 45.881 MHz, while CoreMark stayed at 4,491,485 measured cycles under bus stalls with both DIVU instructions paying their extra latency outside the timed region.\n2:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.99%): The 32-entry partial-tag BTB saved 300,144 stalled CoreMark cycles by avoiding EX squashes on correct predictions, while the forwarded-operand ALU critical path limited median Fmax to 5.538 MHz and left throughput below the round’s clocked-divider winner.\n3:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (improvement, +20.52%): Asynchronous LUT RAM reads removed BSRAM clock-to-output delay from the multiply and branch operand paths, and registering forwarding tag matches at ID/EX let EX use one-hot operand selection, raising median Fmax from 45.918 to 55.338 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count.\n4:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r2s1 (regression, +19.89%): Separately registering the effective address and multiply product at EX/MEM and using valid bits to squash younger instructions removed the multiplier from the BRAM address path and instruction masking from fetch-PC feedback, raising median Fmax to 55.048 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count and exposing the divider recurrence as the worst setup path in placement option 0.\n5:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +15.50%): With EX-to-BTB training registered on the clocked-divider core, the ALU path limited median Fmax to 49.492 MHz, below the 51.64 MHz the 32-entry BTB needed for its 300,144-cycle savings on stalled CoreMark to beat the round’s register-file and forwarding optimization.\n7:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 (improvement, +12.56%): With the effective address registered separately at EX/MEM, parallel threshold subtraction in two radix-8 digits cut the divider’s serial subtract-and-select depth from three stages to\n…[4986 of 8058 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8a5a49ae4a054cc5]…\nEM in timed CoreMark, the time-shared reciprocal divider shifted the timing limit from radix-16 remainder feedback to EX forwarding into issue-time divisor reductions and the ALU and multiply paths, lowering median Gowin Fmax from 104.134 to 103.169 MHz at essentially the same cycle count.\n24:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r9s1 (regression, -3.63%): Resolving the first quotient digit during threshold setup preserved divider latency with six radix-8 or radix-4 feedback steps, leaving multiply-result or MEM/WB forwarding into branch redirect to cap median Gowin Fmax at 100.354 MHz and reduce throughput at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count.\n25:- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r9s2 (improvement, +7.86%): Registering fetch steering from a short PC tag and direction counter let full-tag and exact-instruction replay validation overlap decode, removing the validation logic from the fetch feedback path and yielding a 101.544 MHz median Gowin Fmax at 3,750,582 stalled CoreMark cycles.\n200K\tcores/bench/generated/timing\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
