{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_159", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"date -u; rg 'DONE \\\\(PASS|DONE \\\\(FAIL|ERROR' formal/last_run-wI9KXA6Zw.log | tail -15; git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"", "aggregated_output": "Sat Nov 21 12:19:52 UTC 2026\nSBY 13:12:19 [ill_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:12:24 [causal_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:12:26 [pc_bwd_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:12:31 [unique_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:12:31 [liveness_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:12:45 [cover] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:13:15 [insn_div_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:13:24 [insn_divu_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:18:06 [insn_jalr_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 13:19:47 [insn_blt_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\n# Ready direct-branch repair\n\nID captures byte equality and unsigned less-than predicates directly from the write-first register file. It records sign difference, branch control and prediction, target alignment, and operand readiness on the same edge. The readiness check uses the existing age-ordered EX and MEM destination matches and includes EX load-response pairs. JAL bypasses the source readiness check. The encoded displacement distinguishes a real redirection from a target equal to PC+4.\n\nEX reduces the saved byte predicates in significance order and accounts for a signed comparison using the saved signs. An eligible direction miss replaces the younger fetch queue and ID work while the resolver advances. An older EX/MEM repair has priority, and a data transaction in EX/MEM disables the early path independently of dmemReady. The resolving instruction uses the ordinary EX/MEM path for RVFI and replay training, with its duplicate repair suppressed. The first corrected instruction receives a JAL link through normal forwarding. IF retains the logical correction PC on a missed handshake, including when that PC lies outside the fetch window.\n\nThe two parallel word-address sums exploit the in-window source PC and reconstruct the full logical address when a target crosses either end of the fetch window. The ordinary direct repair reuses the registered alternate PC pair. Separate PC/link/divide and ALU result lanes, predecoding EX forwarding eligibility, and grouping the existing short-offset load address carry were timing refinements. Adjacent JALR consumers use the ordinary MEM bypass after the registered repair.\n\nDirected tests cover all six branch conditions, both misprediction directions, EX and MEM producers, WB write-first data, older repairs and stalled stores, load-response pairs, target alignment, PC+4 targets, fetch retries and the boundary of the instruction window. RVFI checks the exact retired PCs, successor PCs, link and source data, and order. Existing replay tests give trained entries time to publish when an earlier redirect shortens the loop, and the older-repair test uses JALR to retain the wrong-path divide in EX.\n\n## Verification\n\nVerilator lint and all six cocotb runners passed, including 68 pipeline cases. `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and the three random programs in both stall modes. Canonical stalled CoreMark passed all five CRC checks at 3,517,653 marker cycles, matching the probe and saving 47,215 cycles against 3,564,868. Combinational SAT checks proved the split equality and signed/unsigned order comparisons and both alternate PCs against full-word arithmetic for all in-window source PCs and B/J encodings. They also proved the grouped paired-load address sum for offsets 0 through 2.\n\nThe unchanged single-channel formal gate is running. There have been no counterexamples so far.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n| Placement option | Fmax | Worst setup path |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 102.206 MHz | Data BRAM response to the registered load-pair comparison |\n| 1 | 104.022 MHz | DSP product bypass through the integer ALU |\n| 2 | 104.022 MHz | DSP product bypass through the integer ALU |\n\nThe 104.022 MHz median yields 295.71 CoreMark iterations/s, up 3.31% from the champion and below the 105.32 MHz acceptance threshold. The RF-to-predicate, alternate-PC and early predicate-to-fetch paths were outside the worst 25 reported setup paths on all three placements. Earlier routes exposed the RF read and alternate-PC sum as timing limits, and a variant with a mirrored fetch PC exposed the early predicate-to-fetch path.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
