{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_80","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --numstat; sed -n '1,130p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"35\t8\tcores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n22\t4\tcores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n14\t13\tcores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n1\t1\tcores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n7\t3\tcores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n85\t35\tcores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n25\t37\tcores/bench/rtl/replay_train.sv\n125\t14\tcores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Direct transfers at the end of a replay prefix\n\nThe 32-entry predictor and four-slot fetch FIFO now support four-word prefixes ending in a legal direct branch or JAL. A source that lands on such a transfer can install a one-word prefix. The writer learns the terminal branch direction with a two-bit saturating counter, initialized weakly toward the first usable outcome. It forwards pending writes to the same row and registers both resume addresses before selecting one at the RAM write edge. A terminal JAL always predicts taken.\n\nEX/MEM advances enter the collector through a registered event with prechecked address bounds. The collector uses the EX-provided PC+4 and direct target. It stops before JALR, SYSTEM, fences, and direct transfers that EX cannot train. Both conditional successors must fit in imem, and a JAL at the top of imem can replay when its target fits. Its replay PC pair retains the full PC+4 for link writeback. A terminal transfer can also start a new source prefix in the same training event.\n\nThe fetch FIFO samples both lane masks with the predictor payload and exposes the terminal prediction after full-PC and instruction-word validation. ID registers separate taken and fall-through repair enables. EX combines those enables with the resolved direction, and repair owns an independent FIFO refill credit. FENCE.I clears table validity, pending writes, registered and deferred training, and the fetch queue.\n\nThe directed tests check both directions of terminal counter reversal, repair with bus stalls, exact short-tag and instruction-word rejection, and patching a replayed JAL across FENCE.I. The fixtures that isolate source prediction return via JALR so the return itself does not learn a prefix containing the source.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings.\n- Co-simulation passed for selftest and three random programs with and without bus stalls. Canonical stalled CoreMark passed the CRC and MMIO marker checks and completed 10 iterations in 3,679,487 marker cycles.\n- Single-channel formal, the cocotb suite, and all three Gowin placements are running.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
