{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_144","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short && cat implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Target-word replay\n\nThe fetch cursor indexes a 64-entry tagged BTB with two-bit counters and distributed target-word storage. Each entry stores the aligned target and its successor. The four-packet queue stores the bus and replay lanes independently. ID issues from queue storage while IF continues filling free slots during load-use, divide and data-bus stalls.\n\nEX compares the predicted successor with the sequential, direct and JALR candidates. ID registers the shared carry-save terms for the target comparisons. EX/MEM holds the direct and sequential candidates and uses the existing effective address for JALR. The resolved successor drives registered repair, predictor training and RVFI. Training pairs consecutive surviving advances and registers the pair before the table write. FENCE.I clears the BTB valid bits and the younger queue and decode state. Logical fetch PCs outside the instruction window retire synthetic illegal words with their full architectural PCs.\n\nThe first formal run found a liveness counterexample. A divide immediately after a cold redirect exceeded the check's ten-cycle retirement interval. EX/MEM now holds the resolver through the flush and first refill edge, then advances independently of imem readiness. This overlaps the front-end refill, preserves forwarding, and lets the divider retain its original execution schedule. A fetch opcode bit excludes memory instructions from stale taken predictions, so holding a resolver cannot repeat a data-memory request. The liveness check passed on the retry. The full local formal suite is still running.\n\nThe branch comparator groups two-bit comparisons and the barrel shifter groups its shift amounts to shorten the Gowin paths. A combinational equivalence proof compared all ALU outputs and result lanes against the original implementation for every input. The multiplier, divider and bypass lanes use the existing implementation.\n\nThe pipeline tests cover replay during imem stalls, refill during a held dmem request, counter updates, stale JALR target repair, suppression of wrong-path stores and divider issues, FENCE.I with self-modifying code, and instruction-window and alignment cases. The harness checks full retirement order and PC sequences and continues to assert held dmem request stability.\n\nVerilator lint, all six cocotb runners, and single-channel co-simulation passed. The pipeline runner passed all 32 scenarios. Canonical stalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,819,059 marker cycles, with all five expected CRCs, the validation message, and no out-of-bounds accesses.\n\nThe three Gowin placements reached 93.441, 95.128 and 95.128 MHz. The 95.128 MHz median gives 249.09 CoreMark iterations per second, above the 242.034 acceptance threshold. The option 0 critical path runs from a multiplier result to the registered direct-transfer selector. The other two placements are limited by the existing wide divider.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
