{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'ls -a .agents .codex .tmp && tail -n 5 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": ".agents:\n.\n..\n\n.codex:\n.\n..\n\n.tmp:\n.\n..\nagent-logs\nenv.json\norchestrator.log\nriscv-formal-eval\nsandbox-probe\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM into a clocked radix-4 unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. The ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder whose outputs pass through the integer result mux. The divider is the leading candidate for the path depth, so splitting it across clocks could shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and passed UART/CRC validation. It retired 93,960 MUL instructions and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions inside those markers, making the divider a candidate for extra latency with no direct cycle cost in the measured workload.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. The ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder whose outputs pass through the integer result mux. The divider is the leading candidate for the path depth, so splitting it across clocks could shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and passed UART/CRC validation. It retired 93,960 MUL instructions and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions inside those markers, making the divider a candidate for extra latency with no direct cycle cost in the measured workload.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU into one blocking, clocked radix-4 divider on the EX result path. Capture the forwarded source values and unsigned magnitudes at issue, then register the divisor thresholds D, 2D and 3D. Zero-pad the dividend to 36 bits and process three radix-4 digits per clock fo\n…[50268 of 53340 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ae2720f0ed5bb9c3]…\nll three random programs matched the reference ISS in zero-wait and combined imem/dmem stall modes.\\n- Canonical stalled CoreMark passed all five CRC checks and reported `Correct operation validated.` The start and stop markers were at cycles 15,185 and 4,206,526, giving **4,191,341 cycles** for 10 iterations.\\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 45.808, 49.492, and 49.492 MHz, respectively. Option 0 used 3,455 LUT4s and 30 LUT-RAM blocks. The worst setup paths still passed through the ALU. The **49.492 MHz median** gives **118.08 iter/s**, 15.5% above the stated 102.23 iter/s baseline.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T20:35:28.583102Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +15.50%): With EX-to-BTB training registered on the clocked-divider core, the ALU path limited median Fmax to 49.492 MHz, below the 51.64 MHz the 32-entry BTB needed for its 300,144-cycle savings on stalled CoreMark to beat the round\\u2019s register-file and forwarding optimization.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
