{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n --hidden -g '\"'!.git'\"' -g '\"'!formal/riscv-formal/**'\"' 'hyp-20261120-003-r3s2|133\\\\.05|123\\\\.21|4,186,330' cores/bench\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Shorten the ID-to-EX register operand path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion runs at 45.918 MHz median. In the reproduced Gowin option-0 report, the register-file BSRAM launches both the 28.171 ns path through the EX multiplier to data memory and the 28.135 ns branch path back to fetch. Giving the multiplier its own registered stages left the branch path limiting the median to 45.700 MHz. Both paths need faster register operand delivery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion runs at 45.918 MHz median. In the reproduced Gowin option-0 report, the register-file BSRAM launches both the 28.171 ns path through the EX multiplier to data memory and the 28.135 ns branch path back to fetch. Giving the multiplier its own registered stages left the branch path limiting the median to 45.700 MHz. Both paths need faster register operand delivery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Shorten the operand path at the existing ID/EX boundary. Implement the asynchronous two-read register file as inferred Gowin distributed RAM with a synchronous write port. Reset a per-register written bitmap and leave the RAM payload free of reset logic. Return zero for x0 and unwritten words, and give a matching nonzero WB write priority on either read port in the same cycle. This avoids the BSRAM clock-to-output delay on the timed EX paths.\\n\\nWhen ID accepts an instruction, compare each raw source address with the nonzero destination tags currently in ID/EX and EX/MEM. Capture those four match bits with the operand payload. In the following cycle those producers are in EX/MEM and MEM/WB. Qualify the registered matches