ion, +9.49%): With combinational IF/ID, feeding the resolved EX target to imem and the decode PC removed the redirect bubble on accepted fetches and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.91%, with the remaining EX-to-\n…[3931 of 7003 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c9c43e0c98d62c4b]…\n winner.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r7s0 (improvement, +8.48%): Zeroing register-file defaults on bypass hits and selecting the ALU-B immediate before ID/EX lets three LUT4s each combine two masked bypass sources per bit, with a final LUT4 ORing their outputs with the registered default to shorten both shifter and branch operand paths.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r7s1 (regression, +1.68%): Combining ADD/SUB and moving high-word multiplies to MEM preserved stalled CoreMark's cycle count through immediate low-word MUL forwarding, whose EX/MEM bypass through branch comparison to redirect capped median Gowin Fmax at 97.608 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r7s2 (regression, +7.65%): Replaying the first target instruction from the BTB avoided a stallable imem read and cut CoreMark to 3,819,059 cycles, while moving the successor mux after EX/MEM took it off the forwarded JALR add path and left the wide divider limiting median Gowin Fmax to 95.128 MHz, yielding 249.09 iter/s against the round winner’s 251.02.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
