 true; done\nrg --files -g '\"'AGENTS.md' --hidden -g '\"'!**/.git'\"' /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261121-002-r12s1-hypgen\nls -la /srv/hwebench/tmp/5a1ea1b1/96\n39 testbench /bin/bash -c \"sed -n '60,260p' test/cosim/main.cpp; ls -l /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/cores/bench/obj_dir/cosim_sim; rg -n 'GOWIN|gw_sh' tools/eval/gowin.py | head -30\"\n60 The hypothesis is written and schema-valid. I reproduced the champion’s 3,750,582-cycle CoreMark run and found 57,440 adjacent dependent-load pairs with offsets of zero or two. I’ll check how many of those bubbles the proposed capture path can recover.\n81 testbench /bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'basedir|modules/|insns/|cores/' /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/formal/riscv-formal/checks/genchecks.py | head -38; ls -d /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/formal/riscv-formal/* | head -25; sed -n '1,100p' cores/bench/test/_helpers.py\"\n100 The first prototype passed CoreMark with the canonical CRCs and cut marker cycles by 4.91%. Its Gowin median fell to 95.306 MHz, below the roughly 101.0 MHz it needs for acceptance. The slowest paths reached replay training and the ALU, so I’m testing a registered training handoff and a shorter load-address path.\n102 testbench /bin/bash -c 'for v in exact local earlycmp; do tail -1 \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/bench-r12s1-$v-timing.log\"; for p in 0 1; do tail -2 \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/bench-r12s1-$v-timing/p$p/gw.log\"; done; done; tail -4 \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/bench-r12s1-checks/formal/result.log\"; uptime'\n148 The exact address path saved about 184,000 marker cycles and matched the ISS on selftest and all three random programs. Decode-to-fetch feedback is limiting Fmax in several layouts. I’m checking whether a one-entry buffer can break that path without adding an issue bubble.\n211 Wrote and schema-validated the [structural hypothesis](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261121-002-r12s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r12s1.yaml). It uses an older load’s response to capture the next load’s address or equality-branch predicates, eliminating the intervening bubble for eligible pairs.\n\nA scratch prototype passed pipeline and ISS checks and reduced stalled CoreMark from 3,750,582 to 3,565,091 cycles with canonical CRCs. The completed Gowin layouts fell short of the roughly 101 MHz median needed for acceptance. The projected 5% fitness gain remains unverified.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
