{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r4s0','r4s1','r5s0','r5s1','r5s2')):\n  print(d['id']); print(d['implementation_notes']); print(d.get('seeds'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r4s0\n# Divisor-width divider\n\n`cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv` classifies the divisor magnitude in setup. For magnitudes of 256 or more, it seeds the remainder with the dividend's leading byte and consumes the remaining 24 bits in six radix-16 steps. For smaller magnitudes, two radix-8 digits per step consume the dividend with four leading zeros. Each narrow digit uses an 11-bit trial and an 8-bit remainder.\n\nSetup registers the odd multiples through 15D and derives the even multiples by shifting. Carry-save reduction feeds the final setup adds for 11D and 13D. Both paths select a quotient digit and remainder from adjacent borrow flags. The implementation folds the registered width flag into the final one-hot remainder selects to shorten the path into the shared register. The shared phase machine preserves issue, setup, six recurrence cycles, and result acceptance in hardware and ALTOPS. The divider retains the captured RVFI operands, sign correction, zero and overflow overrides, and the completed result under backpressure.\n\n`cores/bench/test/test_alu.py` adds exact quotient and remainder vectors at divisor magnitudes 255, 256, and 257. The vectors include signed operands, a full-width unsigned dividend, and held results at each boundary.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed in hardware and ALTOPS modes.\n- All six cocotb runners passed, including the boundary vectors and existing latency and handshake assertions.\n- Local `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks with zero failures and no failure retries.\n- ISS cosim passed selftest and all three random trace programs with and without bus stalls. Canonical CoreMark passed the CRC and marker checks with both buses stalled, completing ten iterations in 4,491,485 marker cycles.\n- Formal, cosim, and timing ran against scratch copies of the final RTL.\n\n## Gowin results\n\n| Placement option | Fmax (MHz) | Longest setup path (cells) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 74.875 | 13 |\n| 1 | 74.765 | 27 |\n| 2 | 74.765 | 27 |\n\nPlacement option \n…[7001 of 10073 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:494787ec2509dc98]…\n older store advances with imem stalled. They also check exact retirement PCs, contiguous RVFI order, and accepted memory transactions.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL. No correctness fixes or retries were needed.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all six cocotb runners.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and all three random trace programs with zero-wait buses and with both stalls enabled. Stalled CoreMark passed all canonical CRC checks and reported 4,136,096 marker cycles for 10 iterations, down 7.91% from the champion.\n- `python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options` completed placement options 0, 1, and 2 at 73.166, 76.827, and 76.827 MHz. Option 0 used 4,156 logic cells and 1,042 registers. The 76.827 MHz median and the measured CoreMark cycles yield 185.75 iterations/s, up 5.42% from the champion.\n\n[73.166, 76.827, 76.827]\nhyp-20261120-003-r5s2\n\n[70.338, 78.359, 78.359]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
