{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n 'structural|critical path|hyp-20261120-002-r1s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (regression, +742.82%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared five-bit-per-cycle restoring divider shortened the EX critical path and raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 45.881 MHz, while CoreMark stayed at 4,491,485 measured cycles under bus stalls with both DIVU instructions paying their extra latency outside the timed region.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.99%): The 32-entry partial-tag BTB saved 300,144 stalled CoreMark cycles by avoiding EX squashes on correct predictions, while the forwarded-operand ALU critical path limited median Fmax to 5.538 MHz and left throughput below the round’s clocked-divider winner.\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Replace the combinational divider with a shared iterative divider\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench retest reached 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path traverses forwarding, EX, and the ALU across 156 cells. The four 32-bit division and remainder expressions are the most likely source of that depth. A baseline Verilator RVFI trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and canonical CRCs. The only two division or remainder retirements were DIVU, one before the start marker and one after the stop marker. This makes division latency a low-cost place to trade cycles for clock frequency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench retest reached 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path traverses forwarding, EX, and the ALU across 156 cells. The four 32-bit division and remainder expressions are the most likely source of that depth. A baseline Verilator RVFI trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and canonical CRCs. The only two division or remainder retirements were DIVU, one before the start marker and one after the stop marker. This makes division latency a low-cost place to trade cycles for clock frequency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Use one restoring divider for all four operations. On a valid EX \n…[28415 of 31487 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:731071539e5ba065]…\nanonical CoreMark passed all CRCs with both stalls enabled and had no out-of-bounds accesses.\\n- CoreMark's start and stop markers landed at cycles 15,185 and 4,206,526. The 4,191,341-cycle window improves iterations per cycle by 7.16% over the stated baseline.\\n\\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 0 | 5.094 |\\n| 1 | 5.538 |\\n| 2 | 5.538 |\\n| Median | 5.538 |\\n\\nMedian fitness was 13.21 iterations/sec, up 9.0% from the stated 12.12 iterations/sec baseline. Placement option 0 used 12,894 LUT4 and 30 LUT-RAM blocks. The forwarded-operand ALU path remained timing-critical across placements.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T20:04:44.623909Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.99%): The 32-entry partial-tag BTB saved 300,144 stalled CoreMark cycles by avoiding EX squashes on correct predictions, while the forwarded-operand ALU critical path limited median Fmax to 5.538 MHz and left throughput below the round\\u2019s clocked-divider winner.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
