avings on stalled CoreMark to beat the round’s register-file and forwarding optimization.\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM into a clocked radix-4 unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax \n…[32005 of 35077 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9d23559812d85463]…\ntirements per bus mode with 512 operand pairs per multiply variant, signed extremes, and dependent load and store sequences.\\n- `make timing TARGET=bench` and `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` passed. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 53.862, 55.048, and 55.048 MHz. The median yielded 122.56 iterations/s at 4,491,485 marker cycles, about 19.9% above the 102.23 iterations/s champion. Option 0 used 2,998 logic cells and two DSP blocks. The divider recurrence was its worst setup path.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T20:38:31.966007Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r2s1 (regression, +19.89%): Separately registering the effective address and multiply product at EX/MEM and using valid bits to squash younger instructions removed the multiplier from the BRAM address path and instruction masking from fetch-PC feedback, raising median Fmax to 55.048 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count and exposing the divider recurrence as the worst setup path in placement option 0.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
