{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20261120-002-r3s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; [print(n,json.dumps(d,indent=2)[:14500]) for l in sys.stdin for n,raw in [l.rstrip().split(\\\":\\\",1)] for d in [json.loads(raw)]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "3 {\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\",\n  \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM into a clocked radix-4 unit\",\n  \"category\": \"structural\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. The ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder whose outputs pass through the integer result mux. The divider is the leading candidate for the path depth, so splitting it across clocks could shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and passed UART/CRC validation. It retired 93,960 MUL instructions and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions inside those markers, making the divider a candidate for extra latency with no direct cycle cost in the measured workload.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. The ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder whose outputs pass through the integer result mux. The divider is the leading candidate for the path depth, so splitting it across clocks could shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and passed UART/CRC validation. It retired 93,960 MUL instructions and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions inside those markers, making the divider a candidate for extra latency with no direct cycle cost in the measured workload.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU into one blocking, clocked radix-4 divider on the EX result path. Capture the forwarded source values and unsigned magnitudes at issue, then register the divisor thresholds D, 2D and 3D. Zero-pad the dividend to 36 bits and process three radix-4 digits per clock for six clocks, registering the quotient and partial remainder between groups. Each digit\n…[36106 of 39178 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:49a3edb3f8dabbae]…\n recurrence cycles, result acceptance, and captured RVFI sources follow the original schedule.\\n\\n## Implementation choices and tradeoffs\\n\\nThe multiply datapath lives in `ex_stage.sv`, where the product and word enables can share the EX/MEM stall boundary. ID can capture a load-use bubble's payload while clearing its valid bit. The distributed register file and registered forwarding matches remain in use.\\n\\nThe divider recurrence limits all three placements. Option 0 uses 3,432 logic cells, 739 registers, 32 LUT-RAM cells, and two DSP blocks. Its worst setup path crosses 72 cells.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- Verilator lint passed in hardware and ALTOPS modes. The single-channel RVFI contract precheck passed.\\n- The unmodified `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the first run. The run used scratch copies of the existing formal inputs under `TMPDIR`.\\n- All six cocotb runners passed. The production pipeline tests cover the exact multiply vectors, adjacent consumers, load-to-d\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
