d shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and passed UART/CRC validation. It retired 93,960 MUL instructions and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions inside those markers, making the divider a candidate for extra latency with\n…[44812 of 47884 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:17b7097f240f9605]…\nlator lint passed in hardware and ALTOPS configurations.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the first attempt.\\n- All six cocotb runners passed.\\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench` matched the ISS for selftest and all three standard random programs with zero-wait buses and with both stall flags. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical UART and CRC checks and completed ten iterations in 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles.\\n- `make fpga TARGET=bench` completed all three Gowin placements. Options 0, 1, and 2 measured 80.492, 79.136, and 79.136 MHz. The 79.136 MHz median yields 176.19 CoreMark iterations per second, 27.05% above the champion.\\n\\nOption 0 used 4,204 logic cells and 1,075 registers. Its worst setup path runs from MEM/WB through EX forwarding and the integer ALU to the EX/MEM result register. The narrow divider recurrence is the worst setup path in options 1 and 2, close to the EX forwarding path.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T21:20:53.129327Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
