{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\":\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; keys=\\\"id title fitness cycles fmax_mhz critical_path hypothesis implementation_notes\\\".split(); [print(*(f\\\"{k}={x.get(k)}\\\" for k in keys),sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\",end=\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\") for l in sys.stdin for x in [json.loads(l)]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "id=hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\ntitle=Move DIV and REM into a clocked radix-4 unit\nfitness=102.23\ncycles=4491485\nfmax_mhz=45.918\ncritical_path={'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -17.876, 'arrival_ns': 28.171, 'logic_cells': 15, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nhypothesis={'motivation': 'The baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. The ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder whose outputs pass through the integer result mux. The divider is the leading candidate for the path depth, so splitting it across clocks could shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and passed UART/CRC validation. It retired 93,960 MUL instructions and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions inside those markers, making the divider a candidate for extra latency with no direct cycle cost in the measured workload.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Move DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU into one blocking, clocked radix-4 divider on the EX result path. Capture the forwarded source values and unsigned magnitudes at issue, then register the divisor thresholds D, 2D and 3D. Zero-pad the dividend to 36 bits and process three radix-4 digits per clock for six clocks, registering the quotient and partial remainder between groups. Each digit compares the 34-bit trial remainder with the thresholds using three parallel 35-bit subtractions, then selects the highest digit whose subtraction did not borrow. Correct the sign of the selected quotient or remainder on the EX/MEM transfer edge. Carry the original operands and mode for RVFI and for the RV32M divide-by-zero and signed-overflow results. Use the same bounded issue, wait and completion schedule for the existing ALTOPS formulas so the 20-step checks continue to exercise divider stalls, forwarding and retirement. An eight-cyc\n…[38509 of 41581 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5b978e8116505d67]…\ng, target address stability across both ready inputs, and retry after an older store advances with imem stalled. They also check exact retirement PCs, contiguous RVFI order, and accepted memory transactions.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL. No correctness fixes or retries were needed.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all six cocotb runners.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and all three random trace programs with zero-wait buses and with both stalls enabled. Stalled CoreMark passed all canonical CRC checks and reported 4,136,096 marker cycles for 10 iterations, down 7.91% from the champion.\n- `python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options` completed placement options 0, 1, and 2 at 73.166, 76.827, and 76.827 MHz. Option 0 used 4,156 logic cells and 1,042 registers. The 76.827 MHz median and the measured CoreMark cycles yield 185.75 iterations/s, up 5.42% from the champion.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
