{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_76", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r1s2.yaml && git status --short --untracked-files=all'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Predict branches and jumps with a 32-entry partial-tag BTB\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Fetch always advances to PC+4 today. An aligned taken branch or jump\n  resolved in EX squashes the one younger fetch opportunity, even for a\n  loop back edge or return that repeatedly takes the same target. A fetch-PC\n  lookup supplies a cached target in parallel with instruction memory so a\n  correct prediction removes the squash cycle. A 32-entry scratch RTL\n  prototype with seven-bit tags cut canonical CoreMark marker-window\n  cycles from 4,491,485 to 4,191,341 under both bus stalls (+7.16%\n  iterations per cycle). Gowin placement options measured 5.077, 5.482,\n  and 5.482 MHz, for 5.482 MHz median and 13.08 iter/sec (+7.9% versus\n  12.12). The ALU remains the worst timing path. The short tags preserved\n  the observed cycle gain and reduced LUT-RAM use versus full-width tags.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 32-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer with a two-bit saturating\n  direction counter. Index by PC[6:2] and use PC[13:7] as the partial tag.\n  Predict the cached target on a valid tag hit with the counter MSB set.\n  Use PC+4 on other fetches. Qualify fetch hits and training sources as\n  word-aligned addresses in the 1 MiB instruction window. Qualify training\n  targets the same way. Store target[19:2] and reconstruct the aligned\n  address with zero upper bits. Reset only the valid bits so the FPGA can\n  infer distributed RAM for the payload.\n\n  Train only when a valid, trap-free EX control-flow instruction advances.\n  Allocate a taken control transfer on a miss. For a conditional branch,\n  initialize weakly taken on allocation, increment on taken hits, and\n  decrement on not-taken hits. Refresh the target on every taken update.\n  Set a resolved JAL or JALR to strongly taken with its latest target.\n  Store each tag and counter together in a 32x9 metadata RAM with independent\n  fetch and EX reads. Store targets separately in a 32x18 RAM with one fetch\n  read port. Use the pre-edge entry f\n…[1068 of 4140 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:38f1983127694bdb]…\nness margin.\n  Verify both marker-window cycles and all three Gowin placement options\n  on the implementation because routing on the ALU path affects fitness.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: \"Implement the seven-bit partial tags, separate metadata and target RAMs, reset validity, saturating counters, and bounded aligned target storage.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Advance the PC to the predicted successor when ID accepts the fetch and give EX recovery priority over instruction stalls.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose the full architectural successor for RVFI and recovery, plus the trap-free control outcome for training. Compute link and alignment traps from the existing EX operands.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the predictor and compare the fetch PC with the EX successor. Qualify recovery and training by EX advancement and drive the existing pipeline flush on mismatch.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
