{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_54", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r2s2.yaml && rg -n '(timeout|JOBS)' tools/eval/formal.py\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r2s2\ntitle: \"Add a 32-entry BTB with a short EX squash path\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The clocked-divider champion runs canonical CoreMark in 4,491,485 marker-window\n  cycles at 45.918 MHz median. Its sequential fetch loses a cycle whenever EX\n  redirects a taken transfer. The earlier 32-entry BTB cut the same stalled\n  benchmark to 4,191,341 cycles on the pre-divider core, a 7.16% gain in\n  iterations per cycle. At that cycle count, roughly 44.82 MHz median is needed\n  to beat 102.23 iter/sec by more than 4.6%. In a scratch build on the champion,\n  feeding recovery back through the IF instruction mask lengthened the path\n  into decode and limited median Fmax to 41.333 MHz. Route recovery directly\n  to the ID/EX squash and fetch PC controls.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 32-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer to the current radix-4\n  divider core. Read it using the registered fetch PC in parallel with imem.\n  Index by PC[6:2], tag by PC[13:7], and use the cached target when the tag\n  matches a valid entry with its two-bit counter in a taken state. The default\n  successor is PC+4. Predict only for aligned source PCs within the 1 MiB\n  instruction window. Cache aligned in-window targets as bits [19:2].\n  Keep metadata and targets in separate small arrays with asynchronous\n  reads, synchronous writes and reset confined to the valid bits. Metadata\n  has fetch and EX read ports. The target array has only a fetch read port.\n\n  Train once when a valid, trap-free control transfer advances from EX.\n  Allocate taken conditional branches weakly taken on a miss. Saturate the\n  counter up on taken hits and down on not-taken hits. Refresh the target on\n  taken writes. Train aligned, in-window JAL and JALR as strongly taken using\n  their latest target. Require an aligned, in-window source for all updates\n  and an aligned, in-window target for taken writes. A fetch accepted on the\n  same edge as training uses the entry read before that edge.\n\n  Advance the fetch PC to the prediction only when the fet\n…[2393 of 5465 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9cc5ae8255cead25]…\narallel, select mismatch and recovery using architectural resolution, and expose the advancing control outcome for BTB training.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the registered fetch address to the BTB and EX comparisons, and connect BTB training and qualified recovery to the pipeline.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Derive data-stall and EX-hold signals independently of recovery. Send recovery to the existing ID/EX flush and derive the IF/ID mask from imem backpressure.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Cover a trained indirect target change across a data stall and partial-tag alias recovery during instruction backpressure. Check retirement order, PCs and wrong-path side effects.\"\n139:                env = {**env, \"JOBS\": eval_jobs()}\n145:                    timeout=2700,  # 45 min ceiling for all ~45 checks running in parallel via make -j\n168:                'failed_check': 'timeout',\n169:                'detail': (f'run_all.sh exceeded {e.timeout}s wall-clock'\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
