{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_62", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,115p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r2s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r2s2\ntitle: \"Add a 32-entry fetch BTB to the clocked-divider core\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Fetch presents PC+4 while a branch or jump resolves in EX. With no\n  IF/ID register, a correct target prediction can save the single younger\n  fetch slot on each taken transfer. The round-one 32-entry BTB reduced\n  the stalled CoreMark marker window from 4,491,485 to 4,191,341 cycles,\n  a 7.16% gain in iterations per cycle. The clocked-divider champion\n  still runs the 4,491,485-cycle window at 45.918 MHz, so the earlier\n  cycle savings can clear the acceptance margin if timing holds.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a direct-mapped 32-entry branch target buffer to the current bench\n  core. Index by PC[6:2] and use PC[13:7] as a partial tag. A valid tag\n  match with the two-bit direction counter's high bit set selects the\n  cached aligned target. Other fetches use PC+4. Qualify lookups and\n  allocations by word alignment and the 1 MiB instruction window.\n  Store target[19:2] and reconstruct the full address with zero upper\n  and low bits. Use a 32x9 metadata RAM with independent asynchronous\n  fetch and update reads and a separate 32x18 target RAM with one fetch\n  read. Reset the entry-valid bits. Fetch reads the table in parallel\n  with imem and uses the pre-edge entry on a write edge.\n\n  Advance to the predicted successor only when ID accepts the fetch.\n  The current fetch PC then represents the valid EX instruction's\n  prediction. Compare it in parallel with the sequential and computed\n  target PCs, then select the matching result using the resolved,\n  alignment-checked EX decision. This also detects a partial-tag hit on\n  an ordinary instruction and recovers to PC+4 on a misaligned transfer\n  under the existing trap convention. Feed the resolved architectural\n  successor to RVFI. Gate recovery and training on ID/EX valid, data\n  backpressure clear, and divide_wait deasserted. A partial-tag hit on\n  a divide must hold the fetch PC and ID/EX until the result advances.\n  Hold both registers together on \n…[2411 of 5483 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2510f524b9794713]…\nrecovery.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: >-\n      Expose the resolved architectural successor, match the sequential\n      and target PCs against the prediction in parallel, and expose the\n      trap-free control outcome for training.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: >-\n      Wire fetch prediction and EX resolution, qualify recovery and\n      training with ID/EX valid, !stall_ex_mem, and !divide_wait, and\n      connect the younger-instruction squash to the existing ID/EX flush.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: >-\n      Keep imem masking and data-stall detection independent of recovery.\n      Squash the younger ID/EX capture on recovery and preserve the\n      existing load-use, divider, and bus backpressure priorities.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: >-\n      Add the BTB source to the cocotb runner. Cover partial-tag hits on\n      non-control instructions during imem backpressure and check JALR\n      retargeting and trap recovery while older stores wait for dmem.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
