rence was the worst path in placement option 0.\n  A compact predictor that preserves this timing can convert the saved\n  fetch slots into a fitness gain.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 64-entry direct-mapped BTB indexed by PC[7:2]. Store the remaining\n  in-window source tag PC[19:8], an aligned target PC[19:2], and a two-bit\n  saturating direction counter. Use separate distributed RAM arrays for\n  metadata and target, with a resettable valid bitmap. Restrict fetch\n  hits and taken allocations to align\n…[3194 of 6266 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3101539188d01090]…\net when the fetch advances and give qualified EX recovery priority in the PC register.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture the prediction bit with its instruction across stalls, and clear the younger capture on recovery.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Reuse the architectural successor calculation, checking the predicted-taken bit on fall-through and the live fetch PC on aligned taken transfers. Export resolved training information.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect the predictor, fetch PC, and EX resolution. Drive recovery and training from qualified EX advancement.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Keep recovery out of imem masking and load-use detection while preserving dmem, divider, and load-use stall behavior. Recovery squashes only the younger capture.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add predictor source to the runner and cover replacement, changing or misaligned JALR targets, and prediction recovery across both bus stalls.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
