{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'hyp-20261120-002-r4s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; r=json.loads(input().split(\\\":\\\",1)[1]); print(r[\\\"seeds\\\"]); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"[80.492, 79.136, 79.136]\n# Bench divider width split\n\nThe divider captures the operand magnitudes, original forwarded operands, and operation controls on issue. On the setup edge it registers the lane and the multiples of the divisor magnitude. Divisors from 256 upward use the dividend's high byte as the initial remainder and a single radix-16 digit per edge. Smaller divisors use the zero-padded dividend and two radix-8 digits per edge, sharing the low eleven bits of the first seven registered multiples. The setup logic uses carry-save compression for 11D and 13D.\n\nThe implementation uses a common issue register for the dividend magnitude and registers the zero-divisor flag at setup. The selected lane advances for six edges before the result can enter EX/MEM. The captured operands, operation controls, and final lane state hold through backpressure. Directed tests check exact quotient and remainder at divisor magnitudes 255 and 256, both signs, dividends with nonzero high bytes, and the maximum unsigned divisor. The tests check the acceptance schedule and keep a second request pending while checking result and source retention.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed in hardware and ALTOPS configurations.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the first attempt.\n- All six cocotb runners passed.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench` matched the ISS for selftest and all three standard random programs with zero-wait buses and with both stall flags. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical UART and CRC checks and completed ten iterations in 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles.\n- `make fpga TARGET=bench` completed all three Gowin placements. Options 0, 1, and 2 measured 80.492, 79.136, and 79.136 MHz. The 79.136 MHz median yields 176.19 CoreMark iterations per second, 27.05% above the champion.\n\nOption 0 used 4,204 logic cells and 1,075 registers. Its worst setup path runs from MEM/WB through EX forwarding and the integer ALU to the EX/MEM result register. The narrow divider recurrence is the worst setup path in options 1 and 2, close to the EX forwarding path.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
