{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_108", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r5s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r5s2\ntitle: Predict transfers with a region-relative BTB and registered recovery\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The earlier 32-entry BTB saved 300,144 stalled CoreMark cycles by\n  predicting both direct and indirect transfers. Its second asynchronous\n  metadata read duplicated storage for training. On the current champion,\n  a scratch BTB with combinational EX recovery reproduced those savings\n  but exposed a path from writeback forwarding through EX into the fetch\n  PC. Registering recovery cuts that path. Fetching from the registered\n  correction immediately preserves the single-bubble miss penalty. Most\n  CoreMark transfers stay within the same 16 KiB region, allowing the BTB\n  to store only the low target bits.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 32-entry direct-mapped BTB with one asynchronous fetch read and\n  one synchronous write. Index it with PC[6:2] and pack a seven-bit partial\n  tag PC[13:7], a two-bit saturating counter and target[13:2] into each\n  flat 21-bit distributed RAM word. Reset a separate validity bitmap.\n  A valid hit with the counter MSB set predicts the word-aligned target\n  formed from the fetch PC's upper bits and the cached low bits. Qualify\n  hits by source alignment and the 1 MiB instruction window. Only train\n  same-region targets that differ from the source PC+4. This guarantees\n  that a taken prediction differs from fall-through even when the source\n  tag aliases across regions, so fetch can carry the select bit as-is.\n  Advance to the predicted successor only when ID accepts the instruction.\n\n  Carry the lookup hit and counter snapshot into ID/EX with the instruction\n  and a bit indicating that fetch selected a nonsequential target.\n  Register the training record on a valid, unsquashed EX advance of a\n  legal control transfer with an aligned target. Record the source index\n  and tag, resolved direction and candidate taken target, and lookup\n  state. Forward metadata from the two most recent committed table writes.\n  The newest write to the same index takes precedence \n…[2525 of 5597 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:698a8e9c52a88557]…\nwrites.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add the lookup state and actual prediction bit to the fetch and ID/EX bundles.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Present the registered recovery address to imem and decode, and retry that address if imem is stalled. Select the predicted successor on acceptance.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Register the lookup state and prediction bit with each instruction and retain them on a hold.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Register the prediction mismatch and its resolved successor, export training, and gate wrong-path EX advancement and divider issue.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire registered recovery to fetch and the younger EX kill. Suppress load-use from the killed instruction and allow the corrected fetch into ID.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Add the BTB to the runner and cover taken loops, region aliases, cross-region transfers, close writes to the same index, and JALR recovery across bus stalls.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
