d the forwarded-operand ALU path as critical.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 32-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer with a two-bit saturating\\ndirection counter. Index \n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Add a 32-entry fetch BTB to the clocked-divider core\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Fetch presents PC+4 while a branch or jump resolves in EX.\n…[8034 of 11106 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4e71e73f2cc4cdf7]…\ner 32-entry BTB saved 300,144 stalled CoreMark cycles by\\npredicting both direct and indirect transfers. Its second asynchronous\\nmetadata read duplicated storage for training. On the current champion,\\na scratch BTB with combinational EX recovery reproduced those savings\\nbut exposed a path from writeback forwarding through EX into the fetch\\nPC. Registering recovery cuts that path. Fetching from the registered\\ncorrection immediately preserves the single-bubble miss penalty. The\\nscratch run retained the cycle saving with a BTB that stores only the\\nlow target bits for transfers within the same 16 KiB region.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 32-entry direct-mapped BTB with one asynchronous fetch read and\\none synchronous write. Index it with PC[6:2] and pack a seven-bit partial\\ntag PC[13:7], a two-bit saturating counter and target[13:2] into each\\nflat 21-bit distributed RAM word. Reset a separate validity bitmap.\\nA valid hit with the counter MSB set predicts the word-aligned target\\nformed from the fetch PC's u\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
