{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-(003-r9s2|001-r10s0|001-r8s0|002-r7s1|002-r8s1|001-r7s0)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n x=json.loads(l); h=x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\") or {}\n print(x[\\\"id\\\"], x.get(\\\"seeds\\\"),x.get(\\\"critical_path\\\"))\n print(x.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\") or \\\"\\\")\n'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r7s0 [90.569, 104.134, 104.134] {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/mult_1232_s2', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.use_direct_pc_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.041, 'arrival_ns': 16.336, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex']}\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r7s1 [92.912, 97.608, 97.608] {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.write_data_29_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/use_redirect_pc_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -5.763, 'arrival_ns': 16.057, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if']}\n# Keep low multiply on a narrower execute bypass\n\n- EX/MEM carries a selected ADD/SUB word, a shared integer and special-result word, and a dedicated 32-bit `MUL` word. ID registers the one-hot bypass selects and the local register or immediate terms. It also registers the bitwise, compare, and shift enables used by the shared integer lane.\n- EX/MEM captures the forwarded operands and sign bits for `MULH`, `MULHSU`, and `MULHU`. The second operand shares the store-data register. MEM selects the high product into the usual MEM/WB writeback word. ALTOPS follows the same EX and MEM boundaries with the exact multiply substitutes.\n- Only an adjacent architectural reader of a nonzero high-multiply destination incurs the extra cycle. The ISS also checks raw instruction register fields for unused operands. MEM repairs those RVFI snapshots from the older high result without adding a bubble.\n- The timing pass uses two 16-bit branch comparisons. It selects the wide divide remainder within groups of four candidates, then among groups, and uses a shared conditional two's-complement expression for sign correction. The divider keeps its existing cycle count.\n- Cocotb retains the existing multiply vectors and checks all four variants with dependent ALU, branch, and store consumers, including older dmem stalls and held MEM/WB forwarding. It also checks exact operands, contiguous retirement order, unused-field aliases, x0 destinations, and immediate and register shifts.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed in the hardware and ALTOPS configurations. All six coco\n…[6200 of 9272 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e8d2d50806c34706]…\nplay hit behind older queued words when a divide releases issue.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint and all six cocotb runners passed. Cosim passed selftest and all three random programs with and without stalls. The stalled CoreMark run produced the canonical CRCs in 3,750,582 marker cycles.\n- Local formal was incomplete at the implementation deadline. The single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` suite generated 53 checks and completed 8 passes, including liveness, retirement uniqueness, illegal-instruction handling, and DIV/DIVU in ALTOPS mode. The other 45 were pending when the run stopped. There were zero failures and zero counterexamples.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 90.460, 99.606, and 99.606 MHz. The median corresponds to 265.57 CoreMark iterations/s and falls short of the 106.214 MHz acceptance boundary. The longest paths in the median placements run from the issue word to ID result-lane selection and through EX forwarding. A fresh build of the champion reproduced 101.544 MHz on option 1.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
