{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-002-r(7|8|9)s1|hyp-20261121-001-r9s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; ids={\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r7s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r8s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r9s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20261121-001-r9s0\\\"}; rows=[json.loads(l) for l in sys.stdin]; [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"][:6500],r[\\\"critical_path\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for r in rows if r[\\\"id\\\"] in ids]'\"", "aggregated_output": "\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r7s1\nKeep low multiply on a narrower execute bypass\nNarrow the single-cycle EX/MEM bypass to one ADD/SUB word, one shared\ninteger and special word, and one low multiply word. Compute MUL with\na dedicated 32-bit product in EX. Capture the forwarded operands and\nsign bits for MULH, MULHSU, and MULHU at EX/MEM and compute their high\nword in MEM. Forward those high results from MEM/WB, with a one-cycle\ninterlock for an adjacent consumer that actually reads a matching\nnonzero destination. Independent successors continue to issue.\nThis removes two word inputs from each EX operand mux and preserves\nimmediate low-word multiply forwarding. Preserve exact ALTOPS\nsubstitutions at the same stage boundaries as the hardware results.\nDecode the bitwise, compare, and shift operation enables before ID/EX.\nUse those registered enables to select the shared integer result in\nparallel with the link, AUIPC, and divide terms. This preserves a single\nbypass word for that lane and removes operation decode from its EX path.\n\nA scratch implementation passed stalled CoreMark with canonical CRCs\nat 4,148,503 marker cycles and matched the ISS on selftest and all three\nrandom programs with both stalls enabled. Gowin placements measured\n97.995, 99.972, and 99.972 MHz, yielding 240.98 iter/s with six DSPs.\nThe wide divide recurrence limited the median-setting placement.\nAt the measured cycle count the acceptance gate requires about\n100.408 MHz median. Target 100.8 MHz, about 243 iter/s. The scratch result\nleaves about 0.44 MHz to gain and the remaining timing paths make this\nuncertain.\n\n# Keep low multiply on a narrower execute bypass\n\n- EX/MEM carries a selected ADD/SUB word, a shared integer and special-result word, and a dedicated 32-bit `MUL` word. ID registers the one-hot bypass selects and the local register or immediate terms. It also registers the bitwise, compare, and shift enables used by the shared integer lane.\n- EX/MEM captures the forwarded operands and sign bits for `MULH`, `MULHSU`, and `MULHU`. The second oper\n…[15497 of 18569 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:44e1ff8966c2d9c5]…\nch redirect | ALU other result |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 89.982 | 11.099 | ≤10.552 | 11.014 | 11.113 | 10.953 |\n| 1 | 100.354 | 9.926 | ≤9.836 | 9.952 | 9.965 | 9.936 |\n| 2 | 100.354 | 9.926 | ≤9.836 | 9.952 | 9.965 | 9.936 |\n\nWide-lane setup supplied the longest reported first-digit path. The control that selects the wide seed for the first iteration reached 9.876 ns in placements 1 and 2. Branch forwarding set Fmax in all three placements. The source was MEM/WB in placement 0 and the multiply result in placements 1 and 2. Placement 0 used 4,084 logic cells, 1,202 registers, and 12 BSRAMs.\n\nThe 100.354 MHz median gives 241.90 CoreMark iterations/s, 3.63% below the 104.134 MHz champion at the same marker cycle count. It falls short of the approximately 108.93 MHz acceptance threshold.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.write_data_2_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/use_redirect_pc_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.113, 'arrival_ns': 16.408, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
