{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-(002-r13s1|003-r13s2|002-r7s1|001-r11s0|001-r8s0|002-r8s1)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; fields=(\\\"id\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"fmax_mhz\\\",\\\"seeds\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"lesson\\\"); [print(*(str(d[k])[:2500] for k in fields),sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\",end=\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\") for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-002-r7s1\nKeep low multiply on a narrower execute bypass\n97.608\n[92.912, 97.608, 97.608]\n{'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.write_data_29_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/use_redirect_pc_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -5.763, 'arrival_ns': 16.057, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if']}\nThe bench champion scores 231.39 CoreMark iter/s over 4,148,503 marker\ncycles at a 95.991 MHz Gowin median. Its median-setting placements report\na forwarded-operand path into the shared integer result register, with\na product-to-branch path close behind. Round 6 eliminated a mux before\nthe raw result lanes, but each operand still selects separate ADD, SUB,\nand two product words. The stalled champion trace contains 93,960 MUL\nretirements, including 35,640 with a dependent next instruction.\nAll multiplies inside the markers return the low word.\n\n# Keep low multiply on a narrower execute bypass\n\n- EX/MEM carries a selected ADD/SUB word, a shared integer and special-result word, and a dedicated 32-bit `MUL` word. ID registers the one-hot bypass selects and the local register or immediate terms. It also registers the bitwise, compare, and shift enables used by the shared integer lane.\n- EX/MEM captures the forwarded operands and sign bits for `MULH`, `MULHSU`, and `MULHU`. The second operand shares the store-data register. MEM selects the high product into the usual MEM/WB writeback word. ALTOPS follows the same EX and MEM boundaries with the exact multiply substitutes.\n- Only an adjacent architectural reader of a nonzero high-multiply destination incurs the extra cycle. The ISS also checks raw instruction register fields for unused operands. MEM repairs those RVFI snapshots from the older high result without adding a bubble.\n- The timing pass uses two 16-bit branch comparisons. It selects the wide divide remainder within groups of four candidates, then among groups, and uses a shared conditional two's-complement expression for sign correction. The divider keeps its existing cycle count.\n- Cocotb retains the existing multiply \n…[8229 of 11301 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0fa4446bc8334d73]…\nterations/s at 101.544 MHz and\n3,750,582 stalled CoreMark marker cycles. Its four-word replay entries\nstop at the first control transfer and copy all four words into every\nfetch slot. Round 11 saved 71,095 cycles by predicting a terminal\ntransfer. Its terminal counter lookup and training paths contributed\nto a 92.042 MHz median Fmax.\nIn a reproduced champion run, fetch had no word to issue on 267,238\ncycles outside load-use, data-memory holds, and repair. Among the hot\npaths were contiguous eight- and thirteen-instruction loop bodies,\neach entered from its backedge 25,920 times.\n\n\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r13s2 (regression, +2.87%): Keeping the first replay word in each fetch FIFO slot and priming the synchronous block RAM read at entry and wrap let sixteen-word traces save 94,360 stalled CoreMark cycles with zero replay bubbles, while the EX path from the high-word multiply bypass select to the branch-repair registers capped median Gowin Fmax at 101.834 MHz, below the 103.54 MHz needed for acceptance.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
