{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-(001-r8s0|002-r8s1|001-r7s0)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; [print(d[\\\"id\\\"],d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-(001-r8s0|002-r8s1|001-r7s0)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; [print(d[\\\"id\\\"],d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r7s0\nForm the default operands before the ID/EX edge. For each register\nsource, capture the register-file word when neither eligible bypass\nproducer matches and capture zero otherwise. In a separate ALU-B\nword, capture the immediate for immediate operations or the same\ndefault register word for register operations. Retain the raw\nimmediate for addresses and PC displacements and the independently\ncaptured rs2 for branch, store, multiply, divide, and RVFI operands.\nFor immediate ALU-B operations, disable every ALU-B bypass term in ID.\nGroup the six remaining EX bypass terms into three explicit pairs\nper bit, pairing ADD with SUB, other with MEM/WB, and multiply-low\nwith multiply-high. Each pair needs just four LUT inputs to compute\ntwo masked bits and their OR. OR the three pair outputs with the\ncaptured default, giving four inputs to the final reduction.\nApply the same structure to rs1, rs2, and ALU B.\n\nRetain EX priority over MEM, x0 behavior, fallback to older values\nafter a trapping producer, the load-use bubble, and lockstep holding\nof operand defaults and bypass enables under data backpressure. The\nEX/MEM lanes and MEM/WB capture keep their current timing.\n\nThe scratch implementation passed canonical CoreMark in 4,148,503\nmarker cycles and matched the ISS on selftest and the three random\nprograms with zero-wait and stalled buses. Gowin reported 91.249,\n103.152, and 103.152 MHz. The 103.152 MHz median yields 248.65 iter/s,\na 7.46% gain. Placement option 0 used 4,295 logic cells and remained\nbranch-limited, while options 1 and 2 remained ALU-feedback-limited.\nThe acceptance floor at unchanged cycles is about 100.41 MHz.\nRecheck all three placements and the standard correctness gates on\nthe final implementation.\n\nhyp-20261121-001-r8s0\nSplit the current EX/MEM other-result word into two zero-masked banks. One bank captures SLL, SRL, and SRA using registered ID/EX shift enables. The second bank captures the remaining integer and special results. Use one shared other-result tag for both banks. On e\n…[2641 of 5713 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:19ba9cf7964b40c3]…\nlified\nby completion. This moves the divide term off the shared integer\nand special result path, keeps the existing EX bypass fan-in, and\nlets adjacent consumers use the ordinary EX/MEM bypass. Keep\nexact ALTOPS substitutes in the same lane and preserve RV32M corner\ncases, request stability and ordered channel-0 retirement.\n\nA scratch prototype of the divider lanes passed the existing\ndivider tests and canonical stalled CoreMark in 4,148,503 marker\ncycles. Its 100.661 MHz median shifted the timing limit to branch\nresolution with the shared EX result lane nearly tied. A separate\nMEM/WB completion experiment reached only 80.520 MHz and was\nlimited by writeback-to-branch forwarding. Reusing the raw SUB\nlane keeps divide on the immediate bypass and is a timing\nhypothesis that still needs measurement. At the measured cycle\ncount, acceptance above 262.57 iter/s needs a median above\n108.93 MHz. A 110.4 MHz median would yield about 266.1 iter/s.\nRecheck the median and all correctness gates on the integrated design.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -u - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\nimport json, shutil\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build\n\nwt = Path.cwd()\nroot = Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/5a1ea1b1/226d8738/bench-r14s0-_uwsxihw')\nfor mode in ('drain-repair', 'drain-repair-reg-tag', 'drain-repair-early-diff'):\n    dst = root / mode\n    if mode == 'drain-repair':\n        base = wt / 'cores/bench/rtl'\n    elif mode == 'drain-repair-reg-tag':\n        base = root / 'registered-ex-tag'\n    else:\n        base = root / 'raw-disp'\n    shutil.copytree(base, dst)\n    hs = dst / 'hazard_unit.sv'\n    hs.write_text(hs.read_text().replace('stall_ex_mem || repair;', 'stall_ex_mem;'))\n    ex = dst / 'ex_stage.sv'\n    estr = ex.read_text()\n    start = estr.index('  // A resolver is accepted')\n    estr = estr[:start] + '''  assign train_advance = reg_q.valid && \"'!stall;\n\nendmodule\n'\"'''\n    estr = estr.replace('  logic        trained_on_repair_q;\\\\n', '')\n    estr = estr.replace('if (\"'!stall && !repair) begin'\"','if (\"'!stall) begin'\"')\n    ex.write_text(estr)\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=3) as pool:\n    futs = {pool.submit(build, wt, root / mode, 'fpga/core_bench_si.sv', 1, root / f'{mode}_p1'):mode\n            for mode in ('drain-repair', 'drain-repair-reg-tag', 'drain-repair-early-diff')}\n    for f in as_completed(futs):\n        r = f.result()\n        print(futs[f], r['fmax_mhz'], json.dumps(r.get('critical_paths', [])[:3]), flush=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
