{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,240p' fpga/core_bench_si.sv; sed -n '1,150p' Makefile; ls /opt/hwe-toolchain; rg -n 'rs[12]_addr|rs[12]_rdata' test/cosim/reference.py test/cosim/main.cpp formal/wrapper_si.sv; ls -la bench/programs\"", "aggregated_output": "// fpga/core_bench_si.sv\n//\n// FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\n// core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\n// set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\n// synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n//\n// See core_bench.sv for the rationale on LFSR-driven imem, the dmem\n// model, the V2 stall generator, and why the LED observes only the\n// memory-side outputs (RVFI-only logic is pruned).\nmodule core_bench (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic led\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] lfsr;\n  always_ff @(posedge clock or posedge reset)\n    if (reset) lfsr <= 32'h1;\n    else       lfsr <= {lfsr[30:0], lfsr[31] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[1] ^ lfsr[0]};\n\n  logic [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n  logic [31:0] dmem_rdata;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_addr;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n  logic [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n  logic        dmem_ren;\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (dmem_wen[0]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][7:0]   <= dmem_wdata[7:0];\n    if (dmem_wen[1]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][15:8]  <= dmem_wdata[15:8];\n    if (dmem_wen[2]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][23:16] <= dmem_wdata[23:16];\n    if (dmem_wen[3]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][31:24] <= dmem_wdata[31:24];\n  end\n  assign dmem_rdata = dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]];\n\n  logic [31:0] imem_addr;\n  // Single-channel RVFI port set (NRET=1 contract). Connected for\n  // elaboration only; nothing observes it, so synthesis prunes it.\n  logic        rvfi_valid_0;\n  logic [63:0] rvfi_order_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_insn_0, rvfi_pc_rdata_0, rvfi_pc_wdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_rd_wdata_0, rvfi_rs1_rdata_0, rvfi_rs2_rdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_mem_addr_0, rvfi_mem_rdata_0, rvfi_mem_wdata_0;\n  logic [4:0]  rvfi_rs1_addr_0, rvfi_rs2_addr_0, rvfi_rd_addr_0;\n  logic [3:0]  rvfi_mem_rmask_0, rvfi_mem_wmask_0;\n  logic [1:0]  rvfi_mode_0, rvfi_ixl_0;\n  logic        rvfi_trap_0, rvfi_halt_0, rvfi_intr_0;\n\n  // V2: memory-ready signals come from the same stall sequence the\n  // CoreMark simulation uses, so stall-handling logic\n…[9431 of 12503 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:59676582d65257c6]…\n:            'rs1': rs1, 'rs1_rdata': rs1_rdata_pre,\ntest/cosim/reference.py:130:            'rs2': rs2, 'rs2_rdata': rs2_rdata_pre,\ntest/cosim/reference.py:158:        rs1_rdata_pre = self.regs[rs1]\ntest/cosim/reference.py:159:        rs2_rdata_pre = self.regs[rs2]\ntest/cosim/reference.py:165:            rs1_rdata_pre=rs1_rdata_pre, rs2_rdata_pre=rs2_rdata_pre,\ntotal 464\ndrwxrwx---+  3 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 21 12:31 .\ndrwxrwx---+  3 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 21 12:31 ..\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup   2317 Nov 21 12:31 Makefile\ndrwxrwx---+ 14 nobody nogroup   4096 Nov 21 12:31 coremark\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup    135 Nov 21 12:31 crt0.S\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup   6341 Nov 21 12:31 gen_random_trace.py\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup    330 Nov 21 12:31 link.ld\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup 140494 Nov 21 12:31 random1.S\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup 138125 Nov 21 12:31 random2.S\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup 142438 Nov 21 12:31 random3.S\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody nogroup   3684 Nov 21 12:31 selftest.S\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -in 'load.use|load dependen|dependent load|store.data|false dependen|operand.*qualif|response address|carry.select|carry.skip|radix.4|liveness' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n n,r=l.split(\\\":\\\",1); d=json.loads(r)\n print(n,d[\\\"id\\\"],d[\\\"title\\\"],d.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\")[:1600])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -in 'load.use|load dependen|dependent load|store.data|false dependen|operand.*qualif|response address|carry.select|carry.skip|radix.4|liveness' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n n,r=l.split(\\\":\\\",1); d=json.loads(r)\n print(n,d[\\\"id\\\"],d[\\\"title\\\"],d.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\")[:1600])'\"", "aggregated_output": "2 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 Replace the combinational divider with a shared iterative divider # Shared iterative divider\n\n`DIV`, `DIVU`, `REM`, and `REMU` share a restoring divider in `alu.sv`. It consumes five bits per cycle for seven cycles. A single 35-bit register starts with the padded dividend magnitude and shifts in quotient bits. The result transfer applies sign correction to the registered quotient or remainder. Divide by zero selects an explicit result. Signed overflow needs no separate detection logic because the magnitude arithmetic yields quotient bits `0x80000000` and a zero remainder.\n\nEX launches only when the divider is idle and the older EX/MEM entry can advance. It captures the forwarded source operands for RVFI at launch. ID/EX retains the instruction and fetch holds while EX waits. EX/MEM receives a bubble on each unblocked wait cycle so MEM and WB can drain. The completed divide advances through the existing ALU-result forwarding and writeback path. A ready-bus divide takes nine EX cycles. The `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` build captures the existing per-op substitute at launch and uses the same schedule.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed in both the real arithmetic and ALTOPS builds. `alu.sv` includes the guarded core declarations when the alphabetical source glob encounters it first.\n- `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks, including liveness and all four divide instructions, with zero failures.\n- All 82 cocotb cases passed. The divider tests cover fixed latency, response backpressure, reset during iteration, consecutive requests, and reference arithmetic. The pipeline test checks launch behind a stalled load, c\n3 hyp-20261120-002-r1s1 Move DIV and REM into a clocked radix-4 unit # Clocked radix-4 divider\n\n`div_unit.sv` handles DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU. It captures forwarded operands,\nunsigned magnitudes and mode at issue. The next edge registers D, 2D and 3D.\nSix recurrence edges process the 36-bit zero-padded dividend in groups of\nthree radix-4 digits. Each digi\n…[41873 of 44945 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a5f5c4a2217c3512]…\nthe hash, samples the row, then decides what to publish. It skips a sample while an older update owns the same row. Matching outcomes refresh the packet when the source instruction changes or the observed prefix grows. This avoids rewriting identical payloads during hot loops. The collector releases one lane per advance, so each training stage captures just the next lane that could be overwritten before publication.\n\nFetch reads the two short-tag forecasts in parallel and registers the forecast separately from response rejection. A full source check and same-row payload write check govern validation. The first predecoded word is captured from LUT RAM with the source, and block RAM supplies identity metadata and the remaining seven words. The table encodes packet length in four bits and validation expands it into a last-lane mask. The queue tracks direction independently. Two issue-word registers let the load-use interlock control a narrow selector. EX still resolves the architectural path and squashes younge\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
