{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261120-002-r6s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys;p=json.load(sys.stdin); print(json.dumps({k:p[k] for k in (\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"changes\\\",\\\"fmax_mhz\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"logic_levels\\\",\\\"perturbations\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\")},indent=2))'\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 199.29 CoreMark iter/s at 82.676 MHz and 4,148,503 stalled marker cycles. Its longest paths run from the registered multiply word selects through EX/MEM result selection, operand forwarding and the integer ALU to the next EX/MEM result register. The round-5 shared-PC structural experiment still had a forwarded-operand ALU timing limit. The recurring setup limit is the execute-result feedback loop.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 199.29 CoreMark iter/s at 82.676 MHz and 4,148,503 stalled marker cycles. Its longest paths run from the registered multiply word selects through EX/MEM result selection, operand forwarding and the integer ALU to the next EX/MEM result register. The round-5 shared-PC structural experiment still had a forwarded-operand ALU timing limit. The recurring setup limit is the execute-result feedback loop.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Move integer result selection across the EX/MEM edge. Capture separate ADD and SUB words directly from the arithmetic datapaths, a lane for the other integer and special results, and the existing full multiply product in parallel. Carry the one-hot producer class through EX/MEM for MEM to assemble the writeback word. Keep the effective-address register and registered MEM/WB load-or-result word.\\n\\nRegister the full per-operand forwarding selections in ID/EX, alongside the consumer. Compare its source tags against the current EX and MEM destination tags and use each producer's write eligibility on the advancing edge. EX suppresses a misaligned jump's link write and MEM suppresses a misaligned load's write. The load-use interlock prevents a consumer from capturing a selection for a not-yet-ready EX load. Pick the youngest eligible producer and encode its EX result class directly in the selection. Each EX operand is a one-hot OR of the raw ADD, SUB, other integer and special, multiply-low, multiply-high, MEM/WB and register-file words. Register separate ALU B select bits that incorporate immediate selection, \n…[8043 of 11115 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a2f2dd7fec1310b6]…\nle-retire ALTOPS checks on the first attempt with the final RTL. The real M-extension arithmetic was exercised by cocotb and ISS co-simulation.\\n- Verilator lint completed without warnings. All six cocotb runners passed. The suite covers adjacent ADD, SUB, integer, and special results, all four multiply variants, youngest-writer priority, fallback after trapping JALR and load instructions, and forwarding through five-cycle data stalls. Divider vectors include all 255 nonzero narrow divisor magnitudes and exact multiples that exercise quotient correction.\\n- Co-simulation matched the ISS on selftest and all three random programs with zero-wait and stalled buses. CoreMark validated the canonical CRCs over 4,148,503 stalled marker cycles for 10 iterations.\\n- Gowin reported 89.707, 95.991, and 95.991 MHz. The median of 95.991 MHz yields 231.39 CoreMark iterations/s, 16.11% above the supplied champion. Placement option 0 used 4,429 logic cells, 1,318 registers, 32 LUT RAMs, 5 BSRAM blocks, and 4 DSP blocks.\\n\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
