rrence limits\\\\nall three Gowin placements. The worst path in placement option 0\\\\ncrosses 72 cells through two serial radix-8 subtract-and-select digits.\\\\nA small divisor bounds the remainder width, while a large divisor\\\\nguarantees leading zero quotient bits. Use those bounds to shorten\\\\nthe recurrence for e\", \"`cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv` classifies the divisor magnitude in setup. For magnitudes of 256 or more, it seeds the remainder with the dividend's leading byte and consumes the remaining 24 bits in six radix-16 steps. For smaller magnitudes, two radix-8 digits per step consume the dividend with four leading zeros. Each narrow digit uses an 11-bit trial and an 8-bit remainder.\"]\nhyp-20261120-002-r4s1 structural 79.136 [\"{'motivation': 'The bench champion reaches 138.68 CoreMark iterations/s at a 62.290 MHz Gowin median and 4,491,485 measured cycles. The divider recurrence limits all three placements. Its two dependent radix-8 cells put two 36-bit borrow and remainder-selection networks in series. The stalled CoreMark trace has no DIV or REM family retirements inside the timing markers, so shortening this recurrence raises the available clock rate without a direc\", \"The divider captures the operand magnitudes, original forwarded operands, and operation controls on issue. On the setup edge it registers the lane and the multiples of the divisor magnitude. Divisors from 256 upward use the dividend's high byte as the initial remainder and a single radix-16 digit per edge. Smaller divisors use the zero-padded dividend and two radix-8 digits per edge, sharing the low eleven bits of the first seven registered multi\"]\nhyp-20261120-002-r6s1 structural 95.991 ['The first result-lane implementation measured a 79.839 MHz Gowin median. To shorten the divider path while preserving its issue-to-result latency, the final design uses a registered reciprocal lookup and multiply for divisor magnitudes below 256. The estimate is either exact or one low. A nine-bit remainder calculation determines the correction. The radix-16 recurrence handles larger divisors. Conditional negation uses lower-bit reductions on the']\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
