{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261120-002-r5s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys;r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261120-002-r5s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys;r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "# Shared fetch and execute PC\n\nIF owns a 30-bit registered word address and an increment flag. It computes the sequential fetch address and EX's PC+4 from the registered word. An aligned taken branch, JAL, or JALR selects the bus address in its resolution cycle. An admitted fetch writes its exact bus address into the shared register and sets the increment flag. If a redirect advances while imem is stalled, ID/EX takes a bubble and IF saves the target with the flag clear for retry. Older dmem stalls, divide waits, and load-use bubbles hold the shared state.\n\nEX uses the shared PC for AUIPC, direct targets, and retirement metadata, and uses the shared PC+4 for jump links and sequential next-PC metadata. EX sends aligned targets as word addresses. JALR targets clear bit zero and taken misaligned transfers trap with link writes suppressed. The hazard unit separately reports when the older memory operation permits a redirect to advance.\n\nThe first implementation measured a 75.376 MHz median. Its longest path in placement options 1 and 2 ran through the branch comparator to the shared PC register enable. The final RTL shares one magnitude comparison for all ordered branches and uses the branch encoding to select signedness and predicate inversion. This brought the median above the 76.23 MHz acceptance threshold. The longest setup paths in the final build run from MEM/WB forwarding through the ALU to the EX/MEM integer result. Options 1 and 2 still place a multiply-to-PC data path within 0.001 ns of the longest path.\n\nThe pipeline tests check adjacent branch and target retirement, adjacent JAL and JALR link forwarding, target address stability across both ready inputs, and retry after an older store advances with imem stalled. They also check exact retirement PCs, contiguous RVFI order, and accepted memory transactions.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL. No correctness fixes or retries were needed.\n- `make te\n…[2225 of 5297 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:484f6544eb9bbbb1]…\nzero bits to each. Use the shared PC+4 for jump links and sequential RVFI next-PC metadata. The redirect target reaches the register without passing through the incrementer.\n\nThe scratch prototype completed canonical stalled CoreMark in 4,136,096 marker cycles. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 77.172, 79.779, and 79.779 MHz. The median yields 192.88 iterations/s, 9.48% above the champion. Placement option 0 used 4,206 logic cells and 1,042 registers. In all three placements the longest setup path ran from the registered multiply word select to the EX/MEM integer result register. A median above 76.23 MHz clears the 4.6% acceptance margin at that cycle count.\n\nThe scratch prototype passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS formal checks and all six cocotb runners, including the directed redirect and link forwarding tests. Selftest and all three random trace programs matched the ISS with zero-wait buses and with both stalls enabled. Rerun the existing gates and all three Gowin placements on the final RTL.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
