{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-001-r7s0|hyp-20261121-002-r7s1|hyp-20261120-002-r6s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json, sys; \nfor line in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(line); print(d.keys()); print(json.dumps(d,indent=2)[:17000])'\"", "aggregated_output": "dict_keys(['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson'])\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r6s1\",\n  \"title\": \"Register execute result lanes and select bypasses in decode\",\n  \"category\": \"structural\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 199.29 CoreMark iter/s at 82.676 MHz and 4,148,503 stalled marker cycles. Its longest paths run from the registered multiply word selects through EX/MEM result selection, operand forwarding and the integer ALU to the next EX/MEM result register. The round-5 shared-PC structural experiment still had a forwarded-operand ALU timing limit. The recurring setup limit is the execute-result feedback loop.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 199.29 CoreMark iter/s at 82.676 MHz and 4,148,503 stalled marker cycles. Its longest paths run from the registered multiply word selects through EX/MEM result selection, operand forwarding and the integer ALU to the next EX/MEM result register. The round-5 shared-PC structural experiment still had a forwarded-operand ALU timing limit. The recurring setup limit is the execute-result feedback loop.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Move integer result selection across the EX/MEM edge. Capture separate ADD and SUB words directly from the arithmetic datapaths, a lane for the other integer and special results, and the existing full multiply product in parallel. Carry the one-hot producer class through EX/MEM for MEM to assemble the writeback word. Keep the effective-address register and registered MEM/WB load-or-result word.\\n\\nRegister the full per-operand forwarding selections in ID/EX, alongside the consumer. Compare its source tags against the current EX and MEM destination tags and use each producer'\n…[27875 of 30947 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9ef65f9331a77bcf]…\nr lint passed in the hardware and ALTOPS configurations. All six cocotb runners passed.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks for the final RTL. No formal fixes were needed.\\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the ISS with and without both bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark produced the canonical CRCs in 4,148,503 marker cycles.\\n- Gowin placements measured 92.912, 97.608, and 97.608 MHz with six DSPs. The 97.608 MHz median yields 235.28 CoreMark iterations/s, below the 100.8 MHz timing target. The median placements are limited by forwarding from the low product into branch redirect.\\n\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T23:55:18.161535Z\",\n  \"slot\": 1,\n  \"round_id\": 7,\n  \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r7s1 (regression, +1.68%): Combining ADD/SUB and moving high-word multiplies to MEM preserved stalled CoreMark's cycle count through immediate low-word MUL forwarding, whose EX/MEM bypass through branch comparison to redirect capped median Gowin Fmax at 97.608 MHz.\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
