{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '60,160p' Makefile; sed -n '55,130p' tools/eval/gowin.py; rg -n 'DEPTH|RETIRE|LIVENESS' /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep3/formal/riscv-formal/checks/rvfi_liveness_check.sv\"", "aggregated_output": "\nifneq ($(strip $(TARGET)),)\n  RTL_DIR    := cores/$(TARGET)/rtl\n  TEST_DIR   := cores/$(TARGET)/test\n  CORE_NAME  := $(TARGET)\n  OBJ_DIR    := cores/$(TARGET)/obj_dir\n  GEN_DIR    := cores/$(TARGET)/generated\n  ORCH_TARGET_FLAG := --target $(TARGET)\n  # WORKTREE-mode paths (only consumed when WORKTREE= is set on `make loop`).\n  WT_BRANCH  := core-$(TARGET)\n  WT_PATH    := $(CURDIR)/.worktrees/$(TARGET)\nendif\n\nexport RTL_DIR CORE_NAME OBJ_DIR GEN_DIR\n\nhelp:\n\t@echo \"Targets (most accept TARGET=<core_name>):\"\n\t@echo \"  make lint TARGET=v1     — verilator lint over cores/<TARGET>/rtl/*.sv\"\n\t@echo \"  make test TARGET=v1     — cocotb unit tests under cores/<TARGET>/test/\"\n\t@echo \"  make cosim TARGET=v1    — RVFI cosim against Python ISS\"\n\t@echo \"  make formal TARGET=v1   — riscv-formal fast suite (with ALTOPS)\"\n\t@echo \"  make formal-deep TARGET=v1 — full formal suite without ALTOPS (slow, bitvector-correct)\"\n\t@echo \"  make fpga TARGET=v1     — FPGA fitness eval (Gowin Fmax + CoreMark cycles)\"\n\t@echo \"  make timing TARGET=v1   — Gowin Fmax and worst paths (placement option 0)\"\n\t@echo \"  make bench              — build selftest + coremark ELFs\"\n\t@echo \"  make next TARGET=v1     — one orchestrator round\"\n\t@echo \"  make loop TARGET=v1 N=10 — N orchestrator rounds (auto-spawns .worktrees/<TARGET> on branch core-<TARGET>; pass WORKTREE= to opt out)\"\n\t@echo \"  make report TARGET=v1   — per-core experiment summary\"\n\t@echo \"  make clean TARGET=v1    — remove per-core build artifacts (use with TARGET=)\"\n\t@echo \"  make test-infra         — run orchestrator infra tests under tools/\"\n\t@echo \"\"\n\t@echo \"Available cores:\"\n\t@for d in cores/*/; do echo \"  - $$(basename $$d)\"; done 2>/dev/null || echo \"  (none yet)\"\n\n# verilator lint over $(RTL_DIR)/.\n# -Wno-MULTITOP: until phase 2's core.sv lands and instantiates everything,\n# rtl/ legitimately has multiple top modules. Drop this once phase 2 is in.\nlint:\n\t@if ls $(RTL_DIR)/*.sv >/dev/null 2>&1; then \\\n\t  verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+$(RTL_DIR) \\\n\t    $(if $(wi\n…[4435 of 7507 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fdf3f4c1c1b59d5d]…\n\n    tr = outdir / \"impl/pnr/core_bench.tr\"\n    rpt = outdir / \"impl/pnr/core_bench.rpt.txt\"\n    if not (tr.is_file() and rpt.is_file()):\n        return {\"placement_failed\": True, \"fmax_mhz\": None}\n    t, r = tr.read_text(errors=\"replace\"), rpt.read_text(errors=\"replace\")\n    head = t[t.find(\"Max Frequency Summary\", t.find(\"2.3 Max Frequency\")):]\n    m = _MAXF.search(head)\n    if not m:\n        return {\"placement_failed\": True, \"fmax_mhz\": None, \"reason\": \"no Fmax in timing report\"}\n    return {\n        \"placement_failed\": False,\n        \"fmax_mhz\": float(m.group(1)),\n        \"levels\": int(m.group(2)),\n        \"logic\": _cells(r, \"Logic\"),\n        \"regs\": _cells(r, \"Register\"),\n        \"lutram\": _cells(r, \"--SSRAM(RAM16)\") or 0,\n        \"bsram\": _cells(r, \"BSRAM\") or 0,\n        \"dsp\": _cells(r, \"DSP\") or 0,\n        \"critical_paths\": critical_paths(t),\n    }\n\n\ndef _run_gw_sh(outdir: Path, log: Path, env: dict) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Run the build; True if it hit BUILD_TIMEOUT_SEC (its process group is\n    killed).\"\"\"\n", "exit_code": 1, "status": "failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-003-r9s2\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; r=json.loads(input()); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"perturbations\\\"])'; env | sort | rg 'GOWIN|TMPDIR'; ls -l /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-003-r9s2\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; r=json.loads(input()); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"perturbations\\\"])'; env | sort | rg 'GOWIN|TMPDIR'; ls -l /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\"","aggregated_output":"# Steer fetch early and validate target replay alongside decode\n\nThe bench core uses a 32-entry direct-mapped predictor and four fetch slots. A short PC tag, the counter forecast, and the accepted opcode select a registered resume cursor. The next cycle compares the saved full in-window tag and exact source word while ID can decode the bus word. Late rejection leaves the source at the head of the architectural stream and restores PC+4. The rejected response consumes no queue credit.\n\nEach slot stores the bus word, its PC pair, the replay target, and four replay words in flat lanes. A registered issue word mirrors the selected lane, with an acceptance bypass for an empty or repaired queue. The mirror can prepare a first replay word from the early guess because a rejection in that case leaves the queue empty. The exact result controls occupancy, the lane index, and the prediction sent to EX. IMEM can fill any free slot during an ID or EX hold.\n\nThe training collector reads ordered EX/MEM-to-WB advances and finishes on the fourth word or at a prefix boundary. The update pipeline registers the tag match and counter result before writing the table. Back-to-back updates to the same row forward the pending training result. Fetch reads sample the pre-edge table state for both lookups. A matching not-taken branch changes direction only.\n\nRegistered EX repair clears younger work and squashes divider issue. The resolver remains in EX/MEM through the repair edge and can forward its link to the first corrected instruction through MEM/WB. FENCE.I also clears predictor validity and pending training. An out-of-window logical PC uses a parked bus address and retires a synthetic illegal word with the full PC.\n\nThe directed pipeline tests cover short-tag alias rejection, an altered source word, replay during imem stalls, direction counter transitions, repair priority over a pending guess and wrong-path divide, FENCE.I invalidation, and fetch-window behavior. They check retirement PCs and order as well as accepted data transactions.\n\n## Verification\n\n- The initial single-channel formal run found incorrect PC and link results because Yosys treated writes to members of a struct array as implicit scalar wires. Writing the whole packed PC word to each FIFO lane fixed the counterexample, and the rerun passed all 53 checks before the final timing changes. Formal on the final RTL was incomplete at the local wall-clock limit. At the last check, 19 of 53 checks had passed, including liveness, uniqueness, illegal-instruction, causal, backward-PC, and cover. No final-RTL check had reported a counterexample.\n- Verilator lint and all six pytest runners passed. The pipeline runner passed all 33 scenarios. Cosimulation passed selftest and all three random programs with and without bus stalls, plus canonical stalled CoreMark.\n- Canonical stalled CoreMark validated all five CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 3,750,582 marker cycles. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 95.873, 101.544, and 101.544 MHz. The median yielded 270.74 iterations/s against the 262.57 iterations/s floor. The option 0 critical path starts in the registered JALR repair control and ends in FIFO occupancy.\n\n[[0, 95.873, 9], [1, 101.544, 11], [2, 101.544, 11]]\nGOWIN_HOME=/opt/gowin\nTMPDIR=/srv/hwebench/tmp/5a1ea1b1/1d037722\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nogroup 24968 Jul 10  2025 /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
