{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-003-r9s2|hyp-20261121-001-r12s0|hyp-20261121-002-r11s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys\nfor s in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(s); print(d[\\\"id\\\"],d.get(\\\"fitness\\\"),d.get(\\\"cycles\\\"),d.get(\\\"lut4\\\"),d.get(\\\"critical_path\\\")); print(d.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\"))'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r9s2 270.74 3750582 6745 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/repair_jalr_q_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/occupancy_q_1_s0', 'slack_ns': -5.43, 'arrival_ns': 15.725, 'logic_cells': 9, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']}\n# Steer fetch early and validate target replay alongside decode\n\nThe bench core uses a 32-entry direct-mapped predictor and four fetch slots. A short PC tag, the counter forecast, and the accepted opcode select a registered resume cursor. The next cycle compares the saved full in-window tag and exact source word while ID can decode the bus word. Late rejection leaves the source at the head of the architectural stream and restores PC+4. The rejected response consumes no queue credit.\n\nEach slot stores the bus word, its PC pair, the replay target, and four replay words in flat lanes. A registered issue word mirrors the selected lane, with an acceptance bypass for an empty or repaired queue. The mirror can prepare a first replay word from the early guess because a rejection in that case leaves the queue empty. The exact result controls occupancy, the lane index, and the prediction sent to EX. IMEM can fill any free slot during an ID or EX hold.\n\nThe training collector reads ordered EX/MEM-to-WB advances and finishes on the fourth word or at a prefix boundary. The update pipeline registers the tag match and counter result before writing the table. Back-to-back updates to the same row forward the pending training result. Fetch reads sample the pre-edge table state for both lookups. A matching not-taken branch changes direction only.\n\nRegistered EX repair clears younger work and squashes divider issue. The resolver remains in EX/MEM through the repair edge and can forward its link to the first corrected instruction through MEM/WB. FENCE.I also clears predictor validity and pending training. An out-of-window logical PC uses a parked bus address and retires a synthetic illegal word with the full PC.\n\nThe directed pipeline tests cover short-tag alias rejection, an altered sourc\n…[4210 of 7282 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d99df07dbd50987e]…\nn reported.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\nThe retained layout measured 95.642, 98.923, and 98.923 MHz for placement options 0, 1, and 2. The 98.923 MHz median projects to 277.47 iterations per second and falls below the 100.962 MHz acceptance threshold at the measured cycle count.\n\nOption 0 was limited by fetch replay at 10.456 ns, with branch-to-repair at 10.434 ns and branch-to-training at 10.447 ns. Options 1 and 2 were limited by JALR correction into fetch at 10.109 ns, with branch-to-training at 10.086 ns. The data RAM feedback into the effective-address register did not appear among the reported top 25 setup paths for any option.\n\nA trial with unconditional repairs folded into the comparison controls and localized predictor word capture measured 92.556, 96.756, and 96.756 MHz. A further trial moved the JALR successor into EX to shorten its fetch path. Its option 0 measured 91.100 MHz and was limited by ordinary EX operand forwarding into the result lane. Its other reports were pending at the end of the local run.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
