{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys\nfor s in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(s); print(d[\\\"id\\\"],d[\\\"title\\\"],d[\\\"outcome\\\"],d.get(\\\"cycles\\\"),d.get(\\\"fmax_mhz\\\")); print(\\\" \\\", d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"motivation\\\"][:750].replace(\\\"\\\\n\\\",\\\" \\\")); print(\\\" \\\",d.get(\\\"critical_path\\\"))'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r1s1 Move DIV and REM into a clocked radix-4 unit improvement 4491485 45.918\n  The baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. The ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder whose outputs pass through the integer result mux. The divider is the leading candidate for the path depth, so splitting it across clocks could shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and passed UART/CRC validation. It retired 93,960 MUL instructions and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU instructions inside those markers, making the divider a candidate for extra latency with no direct cycle cost in the measured workload. \n  {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -17.876, 'arrival_ns': 28.171, 'logic_cells': 15, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nhyp-20261120-002-r2s1 Partition execute into independent address, result, and redirect paths regression 4491485 55.048\n  The clocked-divider champion scores 102.23 CoreMark iterations per second at 45.918 MHz and 4,491,485 marker cycles. Its reproduced Gowin placement-option-0 report shows two nearly tied setup paths. The 28.171 ns path from the register file to the data-memory BRAM crosses the unsigned multiplier because the memory address shares the general EX result mux. The 28.135 ns path to the fetch PC includes redirect logic feeding back through instruction masking and hazard logic. Both paths cross logic that can be moved off the receiving stage's datapath while preserving EX latency. This matters for the 93,960 MUL instructions measured between the CoreMark markers. \n  {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/dividend_quotient_q_35_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_q_30_s0', 'slack_ns': -13.566, 'arrival_ns': 23.86, 'logic_cells': 100, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']}\nhyp-20261120-002-\n…[7288 of 10360 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a77928b65a6260af]…\nn groups on one side of the existing EX/MEM register and combine them on the other, sharing the same repair logic for ordinary branches and branches fed by a memory response. \n  {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/wide_dividend_quotient_q_21_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/wide_remainder_q_2_s0', 'slack_ns': -5.109, 'arrival_ns': 15.403, 'logic_cells': 42, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']}\nhyp-20261121-002-r12s1 Capture dependent load addresses and branch predicates at memory completion broken None None\n  Round 11 saved 129,329 stalled CoreMark cycles by letting a load-fed equality branch advance on the load response edge. At 101.716 MHz it scored 280.89 iter/s, just short of the tournament cutoff. The champion's marker-window trace has another 59,270 adjacent load pairs in which the younger load uses the older destination as its base. Of these, 57,440 use a word-load producer and a displacement of zero or two. Capturing the dependent address on the same edge as the load data can recover more issue slots. \n  None\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
