{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r10s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r10s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"Classify a legal BEQ or BNE using the destination of the current\nvalid EX load as a deferred branch. Register the two source matches\nin ID/EX and admit the branch without the normal load-use bubble.\nKeep that bubble for other consumers. Let the branch enter EX/MEM\non the same edge that the load transfers its response to MEM/WB.\nA data stall holds both instructions and their operand selections.\nEX computes the direct and fall-through PC pairs and captures the\nforwarded non-load operand. Save the equality of the ordinary\nforwarded operands as a fallback when the preceding load traps.\n\nCompare the saved operand against the registered, extended load\nword while the branch occupies EX/MEM and the next instruction can\nexecute. Both source matches imply equality. Use the qualified\npreceding load write for the comparison and exact RVFI source\nfields. The fallback uses the values from the youngest eligible\nolder writers. Resolve taken misalignment, the actual next PC,\nand the prediction mismatch using the same architectural rules as\nexisting EX branches. Register the mismatch and both candidate PC\npairs separately, then select the correction PC in the repair cycle.\nFeed the existing ordered replay collector one resolved record per\nadvance. Register its repair-cycle inhibit directly from the normal\nand deferred repair conditions, alongside the PC selection controls.\nUse the local inhibit and data-bus hold to qualify collection. This\nkeeps the global repair and queue control network off the collector's\nadvance path and preserves the predictor's training schedule. On a\nnormal repair, release the inhibit while the resolver is still held\nin EX/MEM so its training record is collected once.\n\nOn a deferred mismatch, give the older branch repair priority over\nany younger EX repair and suppress younger EX/MEM requests and\nwriteback, predictor and FENCE.I effects, and divider state.\nHold the resolved branch retirement record through the repair\ncycle and the following decode refill cycle. Release it once at\nthe same retirement point as an ordinary repaired branch. A\none-cycle hold failed the unchanged formal liveness bound when\nthe corrected target was a divide. Keep contiguous channel-0\norder, the same imem correction request and retry, and FENCE.I\ninvalidation of pending training.\n\nA scratch deferred-compare run with separately registered PC\ncandidates passed the existing pipeline runner and canonical\nCoreMark in 3,624,672 marker cycles. Its 99.665 MHz median yielded\n274.96 iterations/s. The longest median-setting paths ran from\nregistered repair into replay collection and FIFO occupancy.\nAt the measured cycle count, acceptance requires median Fmax\nabove 102.65 MHz. The local registered training inhibit is an\nunmeasured timing refinement targeting 103.5 MHz, or about 285.5\niterations/s. Verify all three Gowin placements and the unmodified\ncorrectness gates. A later scratch build with the two-cycle\nretirement hold retained the same CoreMark cycle count and passed\nthe unchanged formal liveness check, the pipeline suite with\ndirected repair tests, and ISS comparison of selftest and the\nthree random traces with and without bus stalls.\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r11s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(str(d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])[:8000])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261121-002-r11s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(str(d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])[:8000])'\"","aggregated_output":"Let a legal BEQ or BNE issue immediately behind a load if rs1 matches\nthe load's nonzero destination and rs2 is available from the\nwrite-first register file. Keep the load-use interlock if an older\nMEM writer must supply rs2 or if only rs2 matches the load. When\nboth sources match, equality is known. This eligibility rule covers\n135,030 of the 135,320 pairs in the champion's stalled trace. Keep\nthe match flags and normal fallback operands in ID/EX.\n\nBeside the MEM load formatter, compare raw response bytes against\nthe already registered rs2 default. Use the load width to replicate\nthe expected byte or halfword and use the load byte mask to qualify\neach comparison. Check the expected operand's upper bits against\nthe required zero or sign fill in parallel with the raw comparisons.\nOnce the low bits match, the expected operand's sign bit also gives\nthe loaded sign. The raw byte comparisons and extension check\nfeed two group predicates at the original EX/MEM edge. When the\nload traps, use the ordinary comparison of the fallback operands.\n\nUse the same two EX/MEM predicates for ordinary branches. For\nequality they represent equal low and high groups. For ordered\nbranches use !high_less and !(high_equal && low_less). Their AND\nrepresents greater than or equal, with signedness applied in the\nhigh group. Use disjoint enables for ordinary equality, ordered\ncomparison and memory equality so their group predicates can feed\nthe register in parallel. Carry the branch polarity for retirement\nand training. On that same edge, register whether to repair when\nboth predicates are set and whether to repair when either is clear.\nDerive those enables from prediction, alignment, validity and\ncontrol-flow type. Fold unconditional repairs into both enables.\nFor a conditional branch, a predicted-taken repair always\nselects fall-through and a predicted-not-taken repair always\nselects the direct target. Use that fact to register the correction\nPC pair in parallel with the comparison groups. This leaves a\nfour-input repair function and an address select in the next cycle.\n\nApply the repair token directly at the IF queue state boundary.\nOn repair, seed the FIFO from imem readiness, flush younger\nvalidity, and inhibit divider launch and result acceptance. A data\nstall holds the paired instructions. Keep the resolver's compare\nstate and PC through the repair hold so it trains and retires once.\nPreserve exact extended load data, RVFI operands and next PC,\ntaken-target misalignment, JALR and FENCE.I behavior, and the\nexisting ordered retirement and replay validation.\n\nThe shared-comparator prototype with local FIFO repair produced\nthe canonical CRCs in 3,621,253 marker cycles. It matched the ISS\nfor selftest and all three random programs with and without\nstalls. An earlier shared-comparator layout reached 94.427 MHz\nin Gowin option 1, led by forwarding into the ALU and the compare\ngroup registers. The measured cycle count needs a median above\n102.55 MHz for acceptance. The target is 104 MHz and about 287\niterations/s. Assess the parallel compare selection and repair\nstructure with all three scored Gowin placements and the\nexisting correctness gates.\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
