{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20261121-001-r9s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20261121-002-r9s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "Use a single normalized reciprocal datapath for every nonzero divisor.\nCapture the original forwarded operands and signedness on issue. In\nphase 1 form the magnitudes N and D and find floor(log2 D) in parallel\nfrom the raw divisor. For a negative divisor, the bit test D >= 2^k\nholds if the raw bits from 31 through k are not all ones or the k low\nbits are all zero. Use those monotone threshold flags to encode the\nnormalization shift without routing through the magnitude negation.\nIn phase 2 normalize D into a 32-bit Q1.31 mantissa Y and register a\n34-bit reciprocal seed S from a 2048-entry ROM indexed by Y[30:20].\nAt index i store floor(2^45 / (2048+i)), saturating entry zero.\n\nIn phase 3 capture P = floor(Y*S / 2^31) in 35 bits. In phase 4\ncapture S1 = floor(S*(2^35-1-P) / 2^34). The complemented factor\nrounds the Newton refinement down. In phase 5 multiply N by S1 and\nkeep the high 32 bits above the 34 fractional bits. In phase 6 shift\nthat word right by floor(log2 D) to form qhat. In phase 7 register\nqhat*D and the sign-corrected quotient candidates for qhat and\nqhat+1. The estimate never exceeds floor(N/D). When the ROM index\ncaptures the entire mantissa, the seed error is bounded by reciprocal\nrounding. Otherwise D is at least 4097, and squaring the relative\nseed error contributes less than 0.251 to the quotient error. The\ntwo refinement truncations contribute less than 0.5. Thus at most\none correction is needed before sign adjustment.\n\nTime-share one unsigned 34-by-35-bit multiplier within the divider.\nLoad its two operand registers with (Y, S) in phase 2, (S, ~P) in\nphase 3, (N, S1) in phase 4, and (qhat, signed divisor factor) in\nphase 6. Use explicit