ves about 0.44 MHz to gain and the remaining timing paths make this\nuncertain.\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r8s1\nPartition the divider by operand magnitude and isolate its result\nCapture the original forwarded operands and signedness at divider\nissue, then convert to magnitudes during setup. Use the remaining\nslots for one of three exact lanes selected by divisor magnitude D:\n\n- D < 4096: use a registered 4096-entry floor(2**32 / D)\n  reciprocal ROM and the existing multiply and correction scheme.\n  Saturate the D=1 entry, handle D=0 e\n…[9454 of 12526 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f4701d63d4b6fdaf]…\np the existing\nraw memory response paths for eligible adjacent pointer loads and\nequality branches. Keep the registered dmem address, EX branch\nresolve, multiplier and divider schedules, and channel-0 retirement.\nA dmem stall holds operands and forwarding flags together. Repair\ndiscards younger lane work before it can write back or retire.\n\nScratch fixed-lane RTL with adjacent late forwarding validated the\ncanonical CRCs in 3,597,805 marker cycles. It recovered 103,349 cycles\nversus interlocking every dependent, and exposed the issue-tag to\nfetch-FIFO feedback as the median timing limit. The flexible add and\nsubtract lane targets the remaining integer chains. Keep the hazard\nterms for late producers and the existing load-pair exemptions in\nparallel to limit the issue feedback depth. At 3.58 million cycles\nthe acceptance floor requires more than 107.19 MHz. A 109 MHz median\nat that count would yield 304.47 iterations/s. Verify these targets\nwith all three Gowin placements and the unchanged correctness gates.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
