{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && cat cores/bench/CORE_PHILOSOPHY.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (regression, +742.82%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared five-bit-per-cycle restoring divider shortened the EX critical path and raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 45.881 MHz, while CoreMark stayed at 4,491,485 measured cycles under bus stalls with both DIVU instructions paying their extra latency outside the timed region.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.99%): The 32-entry partial-tag BTB saved 300,144 stalled CoreMark cycles by avoiding EX squashes on correct predictions, while the forwarded-operand ALU critical path limited median Fmax to 5.538 MHz and left throughput below the round’s clocked-divider winner.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (improvement, +20.52%): Asynchronous LUT RAM reads removed BSRAM clock-to-output delay from the multiply and branch operand paths, and registering forwarding tag matches at ID/EX let EX use one-hot operand selection, raising median Fmax from 45.918 to 55.338 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r2s1 (regression, +19.89%): Separately registering the effective address and multiply product at EX/MEM and using valid bits to squash younger instructions removed the multiplier from the BRAM address path and instruction masking from fetch-PC feedback, raising median Fmax to 55.048 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count and exposing the divider recurrence as the worst setup path in placement option 0.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +15.50%): With EX-to-BTB training registered on the clocked-divider core, the ALU path limited median Fmax to 49.492 MHz, below the 51.64 MHz the 32-entry BTB needed for its 300,144-cycle savings on stalled CoreMark to beat the round’s register-file and forwarding optimization.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +9.49%): With combinational IF/ID, feeding the resolved EX target to imem and the decode PC removed the redirect bubble on accepted fetches and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.91%, with the remaining EX-to-\n…[2916 of 5988 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:84298b14b80b0480]…\nand dmem stalls for issue during imem stalls, with predecoded transfer flushes and refill blocking while a transfer occupied EX preserving same-cycle B/JAL target fetch and the divider recurrence setting the 87.033 MHz median Fmax.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r6s1 (improvement, +16.11%): Registering raw EX/MEM result lanes and one-hot bypass selects in ID/EX shortened the execute feedback loop while preserving adjacent forwarding, and a registered reciprocal lookup with quotient correction for narrow divisors plus shorter branch and ALU paths helped raise median Gowin Fmax from 82.676 to 95.991 MHz at the same stalled CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r6s2 (regression, +8.18%): Registering EX repair kept branch resolution off the four-entry fetch FIFO select and capture paths, letting the queue fetch past predicted branches and JAL during pipeline holds and save 168,208 stalled CoreMark cycles, with the 85.811 MHz median leaving throughput below the round’s 95.991 MHz execute-path winner.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
