{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -i 'load.use|false depend|imem.*stall' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(l)\n print(d[\\\"id\\\"], d[\\\"title\\\"])\n for k in (\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\"):\n  for line in str(d.get(k,\\\"\\\")).splitlines():\n   if any(s in line.lower() for s in (\\\"trace\\\",\\\"dependent\\\",\\\"load-use\\\",\\\"load use\\\",\\\"load/store\\\",\\\"load count\\\")):\n    print(line[:500])'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r1s1 Move DIV and REM into a clocked radix-4 unit\nThe baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. The ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder whose outputs pass through the integer result mux. The divider is the leading candidate for the path depth, so splitting it across clocks could shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and \n{'motivation': 'The baseline Gowin run reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU, with a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. The ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder whose outputs pass through the integer result mux. The divider is the leading candidate for the path depth, so splitting it across clocks could shorten the period for every instruction. A baseline Verilator trace with both bus stalls enabled matched the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-ma\n  dependent consecutive divides, immediate ALU and store consumers, a held\n- Selftest and the three random trace ELFs matched the Python ISS with\nhyp-20261120-003-r1s2 Predict branches and jumps with a 32-entry partial-tag BTB\n{'motivation': 'Fetch always advances to PC+4 today. An aligned taken branch or jump\\nresolved in EX squashes the one younger fetch opportunity, even for a\\nloop back edge or return that repeatedly takes the same target. A fetch-PC\\nlookup supplies a cached target in parallel with instruction memory so a\\ncorrect prediction removes the squash cycle. A 32-entry scratch RTL\\nprototype with seven-bit tags cut canonical CoreMark marker-window\\ncycles from 4,491,485 to 4,191,341 under both bus stalls\nThe integration also separates the EX stall signal from the hazard block's redirect-dependent logic so EX advancement can qualify recovery and training directly. Taken updates require an aligned, in-window target. A trap-free not-taken hit updates onl\n…[15914 of 18986 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:865c9f185253cee7]…\nin placements, execute forwarding into the shared integer result was within 0.005 ns of the longest setup path. The shifter currently feeds result selection along with bitwise, PC-relative, link, and divide terms. Removing those terms from the shifter's register input targets that path without adding a dependency cycle.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Split the current EX/MEM\nhyp-20261121-003-r8s2 Replay a straight-line target prefix from the branch predictor\nchampion's 2K CoreMark retirement trace has 343,792 taken nonsequential\n{'motivation': \"The previous single-word target replay predictor ran CoreMark in\\n3,819,059 marker cycles at 95.128 MHz, yielding 249.09 iter/s. The\\nchampion's 2K CoreMark retirement trace has 343,792 taken nonsequential\\nB/JAL transfers. The first non-control instruction after those targets\\noccurred 322,752 times, while the first six contain 1,134,226 eligible\\nwords when truncated before the next control transfer or fence.\\nReplaying more of each frequently executed target can avoid enough i\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
