{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_45", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261120-002-r5s1|hyp-20261120-001-r5s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(line)\n print(d[\\\"id\\\"])\n for k in (\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\"):\n  val=d[k]\n  print(val if k\"'!=\"hypothesis\" else val[k])'\"'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r5s0\nThe bench champion reaches 176.19 CoreMark iter/s at 79.136 MHz\nmedian and 4,491,485 stalled marker cycles. The earlier immediate\nredirect experiment cut the window to 4,136,096 cycles on an\nolder core. The champion's trace has 294,425 taken conditional\nbranches and 49,367 JALs, versus 13,735 JALRs. Direct transfers\ncan reclaim most of the redirect slots. A scratch implementation\nwith parallel address sums validated CoreMark at 4,148,503 cycles,\na 7.64% reduction. Its branch comparator drove a long carry chain\nand the fetch PC enable, limiting median Fmax to 71.672 MHz.\n\nForm the B/J target and target+4 in EX with parallel 30-bit\nword-address sums of the displacement with the instruction's PC\nand PC+4. Compute the sequential fetch successor independently.\nUse the resolved direct condition to offer the target on imem\nduring the EX cycle. Capture the sequential and direct PC pairs\nand the selection bit in ID/EX, and choose the instruction's\narchitectural PC and PC+4 from those registered fields in EX.\n\nCompute branch equality and order with four parallel eight-bit\nequality and unsigned less-than comparisons. Reduce equality and\nuse the most significant unequal group for ordering. Apply the\nsigned comparison rule when operand signs differ and use funct3 to\nchoose and invert the predicate. In IF, register the sequential\nnext PC and the redirect next PC separately. The sequential bank\nreceives the current PC on a hold or PC+4 on an advance. The redirect\nbank receives the JALR target or the direct target pair's selected\nmember based on imem readiness. Register which bank holds the next\nfetch PC, so the resolved condition drives a single select flop.\nReuse the PC-relative target adder for AUIPC and register the\ninteger ALU, PC-relative, link, or divide result with one-hot\nselection. This removes the extra AUIPC mux on the forwarded\ninteger ALU input. Select the extended load value or EX result\nbefore MEM/WB and register the selected word for both forwarding\nand writeback. This removes the writeback\n…[9087 of 12159 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d25e77bfcf33a5f4]…\nng, target address stability across both ready inputs, and retry after an older store advances with imem stalled. They also check exact retirement PCs, contiguous RVFI order, and accepted memory transactions.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL. No correctness fixes or retries were needed.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all six cocotb runners.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and all three random trace programs with zero-wait buses and with both stalls enabled. Stalled CoreMark passed all canonical CRC checks and reported 4,136,096 marker cycles for 10 iterations, down 7.91% from the champion.\n- `python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options` completed placement options 0, 1, and 2 at 73.166, 76.827, and 76.827 MHz. Option 0 used 4,156 logic cells and 1,042 registers. The 76.827 MHz median and the measured CoreMark cycles yield 185.75 iterations/s, up 5.42% from the champion.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
