{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-002-r11s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | jq -r '.hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-002-r11s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | jq -r '.hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes'\"","aggregated_output":"Let a legal BEQ or BNE issue immediately behind a load if rs1 matches\nthe load's nonzero destination and rs2 is available from the\nwrite-first register file. Keep the load-use interlock if an older\nMEM writer must supply rs2 or if only rs2 matches the load. When\nboth sources match, equality is known. This eligibility rule covers\n135,030 of the 135,320 pairs in the champion's stalled trace. Keep\nthe match flags and normal fallback operands in ID/EX.\n\nBeside the MEM load formatter, compare raw response bytes against\nthe already registered rs2 default. Use the load width to replicate\nthe expected byte or halfword and use the load byte mask to qualify\neach comparison. Check the expected operand's upper bits against\nthe required zero or sign fill in parallel with the raw comparisons.\nOnce the low bits match, the expected operand's sign bit also gives\nthe loaded sign. The raw byte comparisons and extension check\nfeed two group predicates at the original EX/MEM edge. When the\nload traps, use the ordinary comparison of the fallback operands.\n\nUse the same two EX/MEM predicates for ordinary branches. For\nequality they represent equal low and high groups. For ordered\nbranches use !high_less and !(high_equal && low_less). Their AND\nrepresents greater than or equal, with signedness applied in the\nhigh group. Use disjoint enables for ordinary equality, ordered\ncomparison and memory equality so their group predicates can feed\nthe register in parallel. Carry the branch polarity for retirement\nand training. On that same edge, register whether to repair when\nboth predicates are set and whether to repair when either is clear.\nDerive those enables from prediction, alignment, validity and\ncontrol-flow type. Fold unconditional repairs into both enables.\nFor a conditional branch, a predicted-taken repair always\nselects fall-through and a predicted-not-taken repair always\nselects the direct target. Use that fact to register the correction\nPC pair in parallel with the comparison groups. This leaves a\nfour-input repair function and an address select in the next cycle.\n\nApply the repair token directly at the IF queue state boundary.\nOn repair, seed the FIFO from imem readiness, flush younger\nvalidity, and inhibit divider launch and result acceptance. A data\nstall holds the paired instructions. Keep the resolver's compare\nstate and PC through the repair hold so it trains and retires once.\nPreserve exact extended load data, RVFI operands and next PC,\ntaken-target misalignment, JALR and FENCE.I behavior, and the\nexisting ordered retirement and replay validation.\n\nThe shared-comparator prototype with local FIFO repair produced\nthe canonical CRCs in 3,621,253 marker cycles. It matched the ISS\nfor selftest and all three random programs with and without\nstalls. An earlier shared-comparator layout reached 94.427 MHz\nin Gowin option 1, led by forwarding into the ALU and the compare\ngroup registers. The measured cycle count needs a median above\n102.55 MHz for acceptance. The target is 104 MHz and about 287\niterations/s. Assess the parallel compare selection and repair\nstructure with all three scored Gowin placements and the\nexisting correctness gates.\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
