{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; v=[json.loads(l) for l in sys.stdin]; [print(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"motivation\\\"], x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"].get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"]),str(x[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])[:4300], sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for x in v[-5:]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-003-r8s2\nThe previous single-word target replay predictor ran CoreMark in\n3,819,059 marker cycles at 95.128 MHz, yielding 249.09 iter/s. The\nchampion's 2K CoreMark retirement trace has 343,792 taken nonsequential\nB/JAL transfers. The first non-control instruction after those targets\noccurred 322,752 times, while the first six contain 1,134,226 eligible\nwords when truncated before the next control transfer or fence.\nReplaying more of each frequently executed target can avoid enough imem\nrequests to beat the champion. A scratch two-word replay with a\nsequential refill register reached 3,766,067 cycles with all five CRCs,\nbut same-cycle paths through predictor lookup and decode limited the\nbest median to 63.784 MHz. Register the entire fetch packet before ID.\n\nExtend branch target prediction to replay up to six consecutive words\nat a hot direct target. Use a 64-entry direct-mapped table indexed by\nsource PC[7:2] with its full in-window tag and exact B/JAL kind and\ndisplacement. Only a legal, matching fetched B/JAL can use an entry.\nPredict JAL taken and learn conditional directions with two-bit\nsaturating counters. Store the target word PC, replay length, and\nprecomputed PC after the prefix so the fetch cursor can skip the cached\nwords. Store each word in its own unreset, flat distributed RAM lane\nand reset the validity and counter training state. Limit packets and\ntheir resume PCs to the instruction window. On a miss, fetch normally\nand predict fall-through. Aligned JALR