ow-cost place to trade cycles for clock frequency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench retest reached 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path traverses forwarding, EX, and the ALU across 156 cells. The four 32-bit division and remainder expressions are the most likely source of that depth. A baseline Verilator RVFI trace with both bus stalls enabled reproduced the logged 4,491,485 marker-to-marker cycles and canonical CRCs. The only two division or remainder retirements were DIVU, one before the start marker and one after the stop marker. This makes division latency a low-cost place to trade cycles for clock frequency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Use one restoring divider for all four operations. On a valid EX issue, capture the forwarded operands, their magnitudes, and the quotient/remainder and sign controls. Process five dividend bits per cycle for seven iterations, with three leading zero bits before the 32-bit magnitude. At each step, shift the next dividend bit into a 33-bit trial remainder, subtract the divisor, and use the borrow to select the next remainder and quotient bit. Apply sign correction from the registered final state during result transfer, outside the recurrence. A signed quotient truncates toward zero, and a signed remainder follows the dividend sign. Handle zero divisors explicitly with an all-ones quotient or the original dividend as remainder. Return INT_MIN and zero for the signed overflow quotient and remainder respectively. Remove the real combinational division and modulo operators.\\n\\nUse a fixed divide schedule of one launch, seven iteration cycles, and one result transfer, adding eight EX cycles when the bus is ready. Use the same request, latency, and completion logic under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, with the existing per-op substitute captured at launch in that build. This keeps the formal gate exercising the new occupancy and forwarding behavior and leaves two cycles of the ten-cycle liveness window for the ordinary inter-retirement spacing, including a preceding redirect under the wrapper's ready-high buses.\\n\\nHold the divide in ID/EX and stop fetch while EX waits for its result. Launch only when the older EX/MEM instruction can advance. While waiting, let older MEM and WB work drain and inject a side-effbench/programs/coremark/coremark.h\nbench/programs/coremark/coremark.md5\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/coremark_profile_o0_joined.png\nbench/programs/coremark/docs/html/files/coremark-h.html\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
