{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|hyp-20261120-002-r6s1|hyp-20261120-001-r6s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; rows=[json.loads(line) for line in sys.stdin]; print(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\".join(str(tuple(r.get(k) for k in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"fmax_mhz\\\",\\\"fitness\\\")))+\\\"\\\\n\\\"+r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"] for r in rows[:-1]))'\"", "aggregated_output": "('hyp-20261120-003-r1s2', 'Predict branches and jumps with a 32-entry partial-tag BTB', 4191341, 5.538, 13.21)\n# Bench BTB implementation\n\nThe 32-entry direct-mapped BTB uses PC bits `[6:2]` for indexing and `[13:7]` for tags. It keeps each tag and saturating direction counter in a 32×9 RAM with two combinational read ports. A separate 32×18 RAM holds aligned target bits `[19:2]`. Reset clears the valid bits. A fetch accepted on a training edge uses the pre-edge entry.\n\nThe fetch PC becomes the predicted successor when ID accepts an instruction. EX checks that PC against the full resolved successor of every valid instruction that advances, including non-controls and trapped misaligned transfers. RVFI receives the same successor. A data stall holds both the fetch PC and ID/EX, and EX recovery takes priority over instruction backpressure when EX can advance.\n\nThe integration also separates the EX stall signal from the hazard block's redirect-dependent logic so EX advancement can qualify recovery and training directly. Taken updates require an aligned, in-window target. A trap-free not-taken hit updates only the direction counter, since it has no taken target to cache. The guarded type include supports the requested glob-ordered lint invocation.\n\nPartial-tag aliases spaced 16 KiB apart can cause a recovery bubble even on a non-control. Directed tests exercise that case during instruction backpressure, plus JALR retargeting across a data stall, a trained JALR that traps, BTB address bounds, and counter saturation.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with the requested command. All six bench pytest runners passed.\n- `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire checks on the first attempt.\n- Selftest and three random programs matched the reference in both bus modes. Canonical CoreMark passed all CRCs with both stalls enabled and had no out-of-bounds accesses.\n- CoreMark's start and stop markers landed at cycles 15,185 and 4,206,526. The 4,191,341-cycle window improves iterations per cycle by 7.16% over t\n…[13000 of 16072 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5378508ce37dcb00]…\n53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the first attempt with the final RTL. The real M-extension arithmetic was exercised by cocotb and ISS co-simulation.\n- Verilator lint completed without warnings. All six cocotb runners passed. The suite covers adjacent ADD, SUB, integer, and special results, all four multiply variants, youngest-writer priority, fallback after trapping JALR and load instructions, and forwarding through five-cycle data stalls. Divider vectors include all 255 nonzero narrow divisor magnitudes and exact multiples that exercise quotient correction.\n- Co-simulation matched the ISS on selftest and all three random programs with zero-wait and stalled buses. CoreMark validated the canonical CRCs over 4,148,503 stalled marker cycles for 10 iterations.\n- Gowin reported 89.707, 95.991, and 95.991 MHz. The median of 95.991 MHz yields 231.39 CoreMark iterations/s, 16.11% above the supplied champion. Placement option 0 used 4,429 logic cells, 1,318 registers, 32 LUT RAMs, 5 BSRAM blocks, and 4 DSP blocks.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
