{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -m 18 'r7s2|r6s2|r5s2|r4s2|\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict branches and jumps with a 32-entry partial-tag BTB\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Fetch always advances to PC+4 today. An aligned taken branch or jump\\nresolved in EX squashes the one younger fetch opportunity, even for a\\nloop back edge or return that repeatedly takes the same target. A fetch-PC\\nlookup supplies a cached target in parallel with instruction memory so a\\ncorrect prediction removes the squash cycle. A 32-entry scratch RTL\\nprototype with seven-bit tags cut canonical CoreMark marker-window\\ncycles from 4,491,485 to 4,191,341 under both bus stalls (+7.16%\\niterations per cycle). Gowin placement options measured 5.077, 5.482,\\nand 5.482 MHz, for 5.482 MHz median and 13.08 iter/sec (+7.9% versus\\n12.12). Gowin reported 12,766 LUT4 and 30 LUT-RAM blocks for placement\\noption 0 and identified the forwarded-operand ALU path as critical.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Fetch always advances to PC+4 today. An aligned taken branch or jump\\nresolved in EX squashes the one younger fetch opportunity, even for a\\nloop back edge or return that repeatedly takes the same target. A fetch-PC\\nlookup supplies a cached target in parallel with instruction memory so a\\ncorrect prediction removes the squash cycle. A 32-entry scratch RTL\\nprototype with seven-bit tags cut canonical CoreMark marker-window\\ncycles from 4,491,485 to 4,191,341 under both bus stalls (+7.16%\\niterations per cycle). Gowin placement options measured 5.077, 5.482,\\nand 5.482 MHz, for 5.482 MHz median and 13.08 iter/sec (+7.9% versus\\n12.12). Gowin reported 12,766 LUT4 and 30 LUT-RAM blocks for placement\\noption 0 and identified the forwarded-operand ALU path as critical.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 32-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer with a two-bit saturating\\ndirection counter. Index by PC[6:2] and use PC[13:7] as the partial tag.\\nPredict the cached target on a valid tag hit with the counter MSB set.\\nUse PC+4 on other fetches. Qualify fetch hits and training sources as\\nword-a\n…[74337 of 77409 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0825faa556613af5]…\neline runner passed all 32 scenarios. Canonical stalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,819,059 marker cycles, with all five expected CRCs, the validation message, and no out-of-bounds accesses.\\n\\nThe three Gowin placements reached 93.441, 95.128 and 95.128 MHz. The 95.128 MHz median gives 249.09 CoreMark iterations per second, above the 242.034 acceptance threshold. The option 0 critical path runs from a multiplier result to the registered direct-transfer selector. The other two placements are limited by the existing wide divider.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-21T00:06:47.006877Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r7s2 (regression, +7.65%): Replaying the first target instruction from the BTB avoided a stallable imem read and cut CoreMark to 3,819,059 cycles, while moving the successor mux after EX/MEM took it off the forwarded JALR add path and left the wide divider limiting median Gowin Fmax to 95.128 MHz, yielding 249.09 iter/s against the round winner\\u2019s 251.02.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
