{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261121-00[123]-r7s[012]\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; fields=\\\"id fitness fmax_mhz cycles lut4 ff critical_path\\\".split(); [print({k:d[k] for k in fields}, \\\"\\\\n\\\", d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]) for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "{'id': 'hyp-20261121-001-r7s0', 'fitness': 251.02, 'fmax_mhz': 104.134, 'cycles': 4148503, 'lut4': 4286, 'ff': 1346, 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/mult_1232_s2', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.use_direct_pc_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.041, 'arrival_ns': 16.336, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex']}} \n Form the default operands before the ID/EX edge. For each register\nsource, capture the register-file word when neither eligible bypass\nproducer matches and capture zero otherwise. In a separate ALU-B\nword, capture the immediate for immediate operations or the same\ndefault register word for register operations. Retain the raw\nimmediate for addresses and PC displacements and the independently\ncaptured rs2 for branch, store, multiply, divide, and RVFI operands.\nFor immediate ALU-B operations, disable every ALU-B bypass term in ID.\nGroup the six remaining EX bypass terms into three explicit pairs\nper bit, pairing ADD with SUB, other with MEM/WB, and multiply-low\nwith multiply-high. Each pair needs just four LUT inputs to compute\ntwo masked bits and their OR. OR the three pair outputs with the\ncaptured default, giving four inputs to the final reduction.\nApply the same structure to rs1, rs2, and ALU B.\n\nRetain EX priority over MEM, x0 behavior, fallback to older values\nafter a trapping producer, the load-use bubble, and lockstep holding\nof operand defaults and bypass enables under data backpressure. The\nEX/MEM lanes and MEM/WB capture keep their current timing.\n\nThe scratch implementation passed canonical CoreMark in 4,148,503\nmarker cycles and matched the ISS on selftest and the three random\nprograms with zero-wait and stalled buses. Gowin reported 91.249,\n103.152, and 103.152 MHz. The 103.152 MHz median yields 248.65 iter/s,\na 7.46% gain. Placement option 0 used 4,295 logic cells and remained\nbranch-limited, while options 1 and 2 remained ALU-feedback-limited.\nThe acceptance floor at unchanged cycles is about 100.41 MHz.\nRecheck all three placements and the standard correctness gates on\nthe final implementation\n…[10242 of 13314 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:341450daaacec56e]…\ng implementation.\n\nThe pipeline tests cover replay during imem stalls, refill during a held dmem request, counter updates, stale JALR target repair, suppression of wrong-path stores and divider issues, FENCE.I with self-modifying code, and instruction-window and alignment cases. The harness checks full retirement order and PC sequences and continues to assert held dmem request stability.\n\nVerilator lint, all six cocotb runners, and single-channel co-simulation passed. The pipeline runner passed all 32 scenarios. Canonical stalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,819,059 marker cycles, with all five expected CRCs, the validation message, and no out-of-bounds accesses.\n\nThe three Gowin placements reached 93.441, 95.128 and 95.128 MHz. The 95.128 MHz median gives 249.09 CoreMark iterations per second, above the 242.034 acceptance threshold. The option 0 critical path runs from a multiplier result to the registered direct-transfer selector. The other two placements are limited by the existing wide divider.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
