{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261121-003-r[78]s2|hyp-20261121-001-r7s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n r=json.loads(line)\n for k in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"fmax_mhz\\\",\\\"lut4\\\",\\\"ff\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"changes\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\"): print(k, r[k])'\"", "aggregated_output": "id hyp-20261121-001-r7s0\ntitle Fit execute forwarding to a two-level LUT4 mux\ncycles 4148503\nfmax_mhz 104.134\nlut4 4286\nff 1346\ncritical_path {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/mult_1232_s2', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.use_direct_pc_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.041, 'arrival_ns': 16.336, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 231.39 CoreMark iter/s at 95.991 MHz over\\n4,148,503 stalled marker cycles. The Gowin rerun shows that the fast\\nplacements are limited by the path from the ID/EX register-file value\\nor enable, through EX forwarding and the barrel shifter, to the shared\\nEX/MEM ALU result register. The same forwarding network feeds branch\\nresolution, which is within 0.007 ns of that path. A shared reduction\\nin bypass mux depth can help both paths while preserving adjacent\\nforwarding and same-cycle direct target fetch.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Form the default operands before the ID/EX edge. For each register\\nsource, capture the register-file word when neither eligible bypass\\nproducer matches and capture zero otherwise. In a separate ALU-B\\nword, capture the immediate for immediate operations or the same\\ndefault register word for register operations. Retain the raw\\nimmediate for addresses and PC displacements and the independently\\ncaptured rs2 for branch, store, multiply, divide, and RVFI operands.\\nFor immediate ALU-B operations, disable every ALU-B bypass term in ID.\\nGroup the six remaining EX bypass terms into three explicit pairs\\nper bit, pairing ADD with SUB, other with MEM/WB, and multiply-low\\nwith multiply-high. Each pair needs just four LUT inputs to compute\\ntwo masked bits and their OR. OR the three pair outputs with the\\ncaptured default, giving four inputs to the final reduction.\\nApply the same structure to rs1, rs2, and ALU B.\\n\\nRetain EX priority over MEM, x0 behavior, fallback to older values\\nafter a trapping producer, the load-use bubble, and lockstep holding\\nof operand defaults and bypass enables under data backpressure. The\\nEX/MEM lanes an\n…[21409 of 24481 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2b040e466148995a]…\nd per decode cycle, and prioritize repair refill.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'description': 'Carry the fetch PC pair, direct prediction bit, and registered repair information through the single-issue pipeline.'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': \"Capture the selected packet word's PC and prediction alongside the existing decoded instruction and operand selects.\"}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Validate predictions, register independent correction candidates, and annul younger execution on repair.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Wire packet issue and surviving resolver events while preserving the memory and single-retire RVFI interfaces.'}, {'file': 'rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'description': 'Interlock on the valid packet word and distinguish the bounded repair hold from dmem backpressure.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Cover prefix replay and repair under stalls, wrong-path effects, FENCE.I, and the cold-redirect divide interval.'}]\nimplementation_notes \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261120-003-r[456]s2|hyp-20261120-001-r6s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n r=json.loads(line)\n for k in (\\\"id\\\",\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"fmax_mhz\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\"): print(k, r[k])'\"", "aggregated_output": "id hyp-20261120-003-r4s2\ncycles 4202272\nfmax_mhz 64.783\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The 64-entry BTB saved 305,155 of 4,491,485 stalled CoreMark cycles, but\\nits measured 55.699 MHz median on the previous parent erased the gain\\nagainst the current 62.29 MHz champion. Direct branch and JAL targets\\nare already encoded in the instruction. Predicting their direction\\nand forming their target in the fetch cycle removes the target and tag\\narrays and the direct-target comparison after EX operand forwarding.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Use a 128-entry bimodal table of two-bit saturating counters indexed\\nby PC[8:2]. Read it in parallel with imem. Predecode JAL and conditional\\nbranches, excluding reserved branch funct3 values. Form the signed B\\nand J word offsets with fixed bit wiring. Share a 30-bit word-address\\nadder for their targets and compute PC+4 in parallel. Check the omitted\\nimmediate bit 1 for word alignment. Predict JAL taken and conditional\\nbranches according to the counter MSB. Select a predicted target\\nonly when it is aligned and inside the 1 MiB instruction window. Keep\\nthe full RV32 target for architectural resolution. A lookup collision\\ncan affect direction only, since the instruction supplies the target.\\n\\nRegister the same direct target and sequential PC used by fetch when\\nID accepts the instruction. Also capture the selected direction and\\nwhether the direct word offset differs from +1. EX resolves the\\narchitectural direction with the existing forwarded operands, decoder\\ncontrols, and alignment checks. Use mutually exclusive selects for\\nan aligned direct transfer, an aligned JALR, and fall-through. For a\\nnonsequential direct transfer, recover on a direction disagreement,\\nincluding a JAL whose fetch prediction was suppressed. A direct target\\nequal to PC+4 follows the correct fetch path under either direction\\nprediction. Use the existing JALR target and misalignment trap behavior.\\nRVFI reports the architectural successor on every retirement, and\\njumps write the registered PC+4 link.\\n\\nTrain \n…[24480 of 27552 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e840e37a91484660]…\nest and all three random traces with both zero-wait and stalled buses, plus stalled CoreMark. The local simulator build used `NRET=1` from the bench core’s declared retirement width.\n- Stalled canonical CoreMark took 3,980,295 marker cycles, matched all five CRCs and reported no out-of-bounds accesses.\n- The full `formal/run_all.sh` gate passed all 53 single-channel checks on the corrected RTL after one fix. The preceding run found a `reg_ch0` counterexample. A JAL to `0xfff8eb5c` led to a synthetic fetch fault with both RVFI source addresses set to x0 and nonzero data from the parked bus word. The RVFI outputs now report zero data for x0. A directed test supplies a parked word that reads a nonzero register.\n- All three Gowin placements completed on the corrected RTL at 80.621, 85.811 and 85.811 MHz. The median of 85.811 MHz gives 215.59 CoreMark iterations/s, above the 208.46 cutoff. Gowin reported 5,124 logic cells and 1,501 registers. Option 0 alone falls below the frequency required at this cycle count.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
